window.ATLAS_CONTENT = {"meta":{"tagline":"INTERSTELLAR ANOMALY REVIEW CONSOLE","objects":{"3i":{"designation":"3I/ATLAS · C/2025 N1","loebScale":3,"pillNote":"REVIEW ACTIVE","loebScaleHistory":"RANK 4 JUL 2025 · HELD 4 DEC 2025 · REDUCED TO 3 MAR 2026 (QUIET JUPITER PASS)","anomalyCountNote":"LOEB'S FINAL PUBLISHED TALLY: 22 (2026-03-12) — REGISTER TRACKS 25 GRANULAR CASES","datasetVerify":"TIMELINE DATASET: CORRECTED · COMPARISON DATASET: CORRECTED · ANOMALY CASES INDIVIDUALLY FACT-CHECKED"},"1i":{"designation":"1I/'OUMUAMUA · 1I/2017 U1","loebScale":4,"pillNote":"RETROSPECTIVE FILE","loebScaleHistory":"THE ORIGINAL CASE — BIALY-LOEB LIGHTSAIL 2018 · RETRO-RANKED ON THE 2025 LOEB SCALE","anomalyCountNote":"11 CASES ON FILE","datasetVerify":"TIMELINE DATASET: CORRECTED · ANOMALY CASES INDIVIDUALLY FACT-CHECKED"},"2i":{"designation":"2I/BORISOV · C/2019 Q4","loebScale":0,"pillNote":"NATURAL CONTROL","loebScaleHistory":"THE CONTROL CASE — AN ORDINARY COMET FROM ANOTHER STAR. FILE THIN BY DESIGN.","anomalyCountNote":"5 NOTABLE ITEMS","datasetVerify":"TIMELINE DATASET: CORRECTED · ITEMS INDIVIDUALLY FACT-CHECKED"}}},"anomalies":[{"object":"3i","id":"A-01","title":"Anomalously large size / interstellar mass budget","date":"2025-07-08","observation":"Early photometry made 3I/ATLAS far brighter than expected for an interstellar object at ~4.5 AU: if the light came from a bare nucleus (albedo ~0.05), its radius would be ~10 km (~20 km diameter) — a mass Loeb put at roughly a million times 'Oumuamua's and a thousand times Borisov's (his July paper says over ten-million-fold and hundred-thousand-fold vs Borisov's core). Later HST/JWST work capped the nucleus far smaller (Jewitt et al. HST: radius ~0.22-2.8 km; Loeb's final essay uses a 2.6 km diameter). A separate September 2025 Loeb-Cloete-Veres analysis claimed the absence of pre-perihelion non-gravitational acceleration independently forced a mass above ~33 billion tons (diameter >5 km).","loeb_take":"Loeb (arXiv:2507.05881, submitted 2025-07-08) argued the interstellar rocky-material reservoir cannot deliver ~20-km objects at this detection rate — one would expect millions of 'Oumuamua-sized objects for each object of 3I/ATLAS's mass scale, so it is either a comet with a small core (<0.6 km) or a member of an implausibly rare population exceeding the interstellar mass budget. In his November 2025 likelihood ranking he assigned the mass anomaly P<0.001, and he kept the 'mass budget discrepancy' as anomaly #1 of his final 22-item list, arguing planetary-disk reservoirs miss the required mass by orders of magnitude even for the revised 2.6-km diameter.","loeb_quote":"[paraphrase] The inferred nucleus diameter of 2.6 kilometers and the number density of its parent population (assuming a natural comet) exceed the mass reservoir of planetary disks around low-metallicity stars by orders of magnitude.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"The brightness is dominated by a dust coma, not the nucleus; Hubble imaging (Jewitt et al., Aug 2025, arXiv:2508.02934) constrained the nucleus radius to <2.8 km (albedo 0.04, lower bound ~0.22 km), eliminating the population problem. Kilometer-scale interstellar comets are consistent with expected ISO size distributions, and Rubin Observatory is expected to find many more. Jason Wright (AstroWright) notes Loeb's later 33-billion-ton mass floor rested on assuming non-gravitational-acceleration measurement precision of 1e-10 AU/day^2 when the actual precision is ~1e-7 — off by ~1000x.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.05881","https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02934","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-02","title":"Ecliptic-plane alignment (~5 deg, 0.2%)","date":"2025-07-16","observation":"3I/ATLAS's retrograde hyperbolic trajectory (inclination ~175 deg) lies within about 5 degrees of the ecliptic — the orbital plane of the planets. For a randomly oriented incoming interstellar trajectory, Loeb computed a ~0.2% chance of such alignment.","loeb_take":"In the July 2025 paper 'Is the Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS Alien Technology?' (Hibberd, Crowl, Loeb; arXiv v1 submitted July 16, 2025), this was the founding anomaly: a low retrograde tilt to the ecliptic 'offers various benefits to an Extra-terrestrial Intelligence,' making the path look like a designed reconnaissance trajectory with optimal access to the inner planets. After introducing the Loeb scale in August 2025 (arXiv:2508.09167), Loeb kept this among the core set anchoring his rating — 'there are eight anomalies that endow it with a rank of 4 on the Loeb scale.'","loeb_quote":"aligned to within 5 degrees with the orbital plane of the planets","quote_source":"'3I/ATLAS is Fading Away, Leaving Us to Ponder Over Its 22 Mysterious Anomalies', Medium, March 12, 2026","official_explanation":"Mainstream astronomers call this a textbook a-posteriori probability fallacy: the specific property was chosen after observing it ('he chose specific aspects of the comet's orbit to compute probabilities for after he knew what they were' — Jason Wright), and with any object some ~0.2%-level coincidences are guaranteed among the many orbital parameters one could examine. AstroWright (Nov 9, 2025) labels it a 'classic fallacy and misuse of probability theory,' citing Hector Socas-Navarro's detailed critique; there is also mild survey selection bias toward ecliptic discoveries. Dynamically the orbit is unremarkable for a galactic-disk object.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12213","https://lweb.cfa.harvard.edu/~loeb/HCL25.pdf","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.09167"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-03","title":"Fine-tuned arrival geometry (Mars/Venus/Jupiter passes)","date":"2025-07-16","observation":"The arrival time placed 3I/ATLAS within tens of millions of km of three planets: ~29 million km (0.19 AU) from Mars on Oct 3, 2025, ~0.65 AU from Venus in November, and 53.6 million km (0.358 AU) from Jupiter on Mar 16, 2026. Loeb estimated a combined probability of order 0.005% (he later listed P=0.00005) for a random interstellar object to thread this sequence.","loeb_take":"In the July 2025 paper and later essays, Loeb argued the timing looks 'fine-tuned' for planetary reconnaissance — exactly the flyby program a probe designer would choose. This was one of the original 8 anomalies behind his rank-4 assignment and remained #3 in his final 22-item list.","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Astronomers respond that any object traveling in the ecliptic plane will necessarily pass 'near' planets, so the claim double-counts the ecliptic-alignment anomaly; AstroWright calls it question-begging, noting Loeb treats both close approaches and non-close approaches as anomalous. Tens of millions of km are distant passes with no operational significance. NASA states all evidence points to a natural comet.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://lweb.cfa.harvard.edu/~loeb/HCL25.pdf","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-04","title":"Perihelion hidden behind the Sun","date":"2025-07-16","observation":"Perihelion occurred Oct 29, 2025 (Oct 29-30 across time zones) at 1.356 AU while 3I/ATLAS was on the far side of the Sun as seen from Earth (solar conjunction Oct 21) — unobservable by ground-based telescopes from late September until it re-emerged in early December 2025.","loeb_take":"In the July 16, 2025 arXiv paper (Hibberd, Crowl & Loeb, arXiv:2507.12213), Loeb and coauthors flagged that this geometry would let a technological probe execute a clandestine reverse Solar Oberth maneuver — an optimal braking burn at maximum solar depth — while hidden from Earth. In his ranked anomaly lists this is folded into the 'fine-tuned arrival time' anomaly (P=0.00005, Nov 23, 2025 Medium list), and he explicitly called for post-conjunction astrometry to check for maneuvers.","loeb_quote":"The eclipse by the Sun from Earth of 3I/ATLAS at perihelion, would allow it to conduct a clandestine reverse Solar Oberth Manoeuvre, an optimal high-thrust strategy for interstellar spacecraft to brake and stay bound to the Sun.","quote_source":"Hibberd, Crowl & Loeb, 'Is the Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS Alien Technology?', arXiv:2507.12213 (submitted Jul 16, 2025)","official_explanation":"Perihelion viewing geometry is set by chance orbital phase; solar conjunctions like this are routine for comets. Moreover the object was NOT unobserved: STEREO (Sep 11-Oct 2), SOHO/LASCO (Oct 16-26), GOES-19 CCOR-1, PUNCH, and Parker Solar Probe's WISPR (Oct 15-Nov 5) imaged it through conjunction and perihelion, Mars orbiters (MRO/HiRISE, ExoMars TGO) imaged it at the ~Oct 3 Mars flyby, and Europa Clipper observed it Nov 6. It re-emerged on a trajectory consistent with a natural comet; the small (~3.7-sigma) non-gravitational acceleration reported by JPL/MPC is attributed by most astronomers to ordinary cometary outgassing.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"timing","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12213","https://lweb.cfa.harvard.edu/~loeb/HCL25.pdf","https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/3i-atlas/","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-05","title":"Sunward anti-tail / collimated sunward jet","date":"2025-08-05","observation":"The Hubble image of July 21, 2025 showed the dust distribution extended toward the Sun, about ten times longer than wide, with no classical dust tail; a sunward anti-tail re-appeared in July-August and again in November 2025, eventually reaching ~1 million km. Mars HiRISE imaging on Oct 2, 2025 also showed a glowing extension ahead of the object along its direction of motion.","loeb_take":"Loeb argued this is a genuine sunward-pointing jet, not the perspective illusion seen in thousands of normal comets (P<0.001 in his Nov 23, 2025 likelihood-ordered list), and that sustaining a collimated sunward plume is more naturally explained by a thruster pointed at the Sun for braking than by sublimating ice.","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Anti-tails and sunward fans are well-documented comet phenomena; Jewitt's HST modeling explains the geometry with large, slow-moving dust grains ejected from the sunlit hemisphere that lag the nucleus, combined with projection effects. AstroWright notes the underlying mechanism has been understood for ~50 years and similar features appear in comets like 17P/Holmes and C/2016 R2.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","https://thedebrief.org/3i-atlas-displays-more-anomalies-as-latest-data-confirms-strange-object-has-developed-an-anti-solar-tail/","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-06","title":"CO2-dominated coma; water only ~4% of plume","date":"2025-08-25","observation":"JWST NIRSpec observations of Aug 6, 2025 at 3.32 AU (Cordiner et al., arXiv:2508.18209) found a carbon-dioxide-dominated gas coma with CO2/H2O = 7.6 +/- 0.3 (~8:1) — among the highest ratios ever observed in any comet, ~4.5 sigma above the trend for solar-system comets (survey median ~0.12). By mass the plume was ~87% CO2, ~9% CO, and only ~4% water.","loeb_take":"Loeb listed the near-absence of water — the defining volatile of familiar comets — as a minor anomaly at P<0.1 in his 13-item likelihood-ranked list (Nov 23, 2025), suggesting the 'gas plume' might not be cometary sublimation at all but sunlight releasing ices and dust accumulated on the surface of a technological object during its interstellar journey. It is not a separate item in his final 22-anomaly list (Mar 12, 2026), compiled after post-perihelion water detections.","loeb_quote":"The gas plume surrounding 3I/ATLAS contains only 4% water by mass, a dominant constituent of familiar solar-system comets.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","official_explanation":"A CO2-rich nucleus is natural for a body formed near its parent disk's CO2 snowline or processed by galactic cosmic rays over billions of years (arXiv:2510.26308); at >3 AU water sublimates inefficiently while CO2 vents freely. Water/OH production rose strongly closer to the Sun — MeerKAT detected 1665/1667 MHz OH absorption on Oct 24, 2025 (ATel #17473), and SOHO/SWAN measured ~3.2e29 water molecules/s near perihelion — and interstellar comets are expected to show compositional diversity.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18209","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","https://www.universetoday.com/articles/3iatlass-coma-is-largely-carbon-dioxide","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-07","title":"Nickel emission without iron (nickel-carbonyl signature)","date":"2025-08-28","observation":"VLT/UVES spectroscopy (early Jul-Aug 21, 2025; ~4.4 to 2.85 AU inbound) detected strong atomic nickel (Ni I) emission with iron (Fe I) undetected — by Aug 21 the Ni production rate was 4.6 +/- 0.7 g/s versus CN at 17.6 +/- 2.0 g/s, rising steeply (Q(Ni) proportional to r^-7.7). Keck II/KCWI imaging (Aug 24, published Oct 2025) confirmed the Ni is concentrated within ~600 km of the nucleus (e-folding radius 593.7 +/- 14.8 km vs 841 km for CN). The Ni/CN ratio (~0.1) is orders of magnitude above the solar-system comet median and higher than 2I/Borisov.","loeb_take":"Loeb stressed that release of nickel without iron matches the industrial nickel-refining process via nickel tetracarbonyl, Ni(CO)4 — writing that nickel without iron 'is a signature of industrial production of nickel alloys' and that the carbonyl channel is 'an extremely rare and exotic possibility in comets, whereas it is a standard technology for industrial nickel refining.'","loeb_quote":"is a signature of industrial production of nickel alloys","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'A Steeply-Rising Production of Cyanide and Nickel Without Iron in the Gas Plume Around 3I/ATLAS', Medium, Aug 28, 2025 (verified)","official_explanation":"Ni and Fe emission had already been detected together (at roughly equal abundance) in ~20 solar-system comets and in 2I/Borisov (Manfroid et al. 2021), with low-temperature carbonyl/metal-organic sublimation a leading natural mechanism. For 3I/ATLAS, the observing teams themselves proposed natural in-situ Ni(CO)4 (or Ni-organic/PAH complex) formation and photodissociation near the nucleus — which predicts exactly the observed central concentration — with Fe carbonyl being less volatile and Fe I lines harder to excite/detect at large distances. Fe was subsequently detected as 3I/ATLAS approached the Sun (arXiv 2509.26053), and the Ni/Fe ratio dropped rapidly with decreasing heliocentric distance, trending toward normal comet values. Astronomers (e.g. Jason Wright) call it an extreme but chemically explicable value, not evidence of artificial nickel plating.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.18382","https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11779","https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.26053","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-steeply-rising-production-of-cyanide-and-nickel-without-iron-in-the-gas-plume-around-3i-atlas-536e20674303"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-08","title":"Extreme negative polarization","date":"2025-09-05","observation":"Pre-perihelion polarimetry with FORS2/VLT, ALFOSC/NOT and FoReRo2 (phase angles 7.7-22.4 deg) revealed an unprecedentedly deep, narrow negative polarization branch: minimum -2.7% at 7 deg phase angle with an inversion angle of ~17 deg — a combination never seen in any asteroid or comet, including 2I/Borisov (arXiv:2509.05181, Sept 5, 2025).","loeb_take":"Loeb listed this at P<0.01 as a medium anomaly: light-scattering behavior unprecedented among all known comets, possibly related to the anomalous sunward anti-tail, and another entry in a chain of coincidences whose combined probability he multiplied to argue 3I/ATLAS is 'extremely rare and mysterious'. It stayed as #21 in his final 22-item list.","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"The measuring team's own conclusion is natural: the polarization is extreme but continuous with behavior of trans-Neptunian objects and Centaur Pholus, implying fine, possibly water-ice-bearing aggregate dust — evidence 3I is a distinct type of comet, expanding known diversity. AstroWright: negative polarization itself is normal for comets; this is just a stronger case, and no one has proposed why a spacecraft would produce it.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"polarization","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05181","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/extreme-negative-polarization-of-3i-atlas-75d01d13d5c7","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-09","title":"Arrival direction matches the 1977 Wow! Signal","date":"2025-09-28","observation":"Loeb noted that 3I/ATLAS's back-traced position in mid-August 1977 (RA 19h40m = 295 deg, Dec -19 deg) lies ~4 deg in RA and 8 deg in Dec (~9 deg total) from the direction of the famous narrowband 'Wow! Signal' detected by Ohio State's Big Ear telescope on Aug 15, 1977 (RA 19h25m = 291 deg, Dec -27 deg). He put the chance of two random sky directions aligning that closely at ~0.6% (P=0.006).","loeb_take":"In 'Was the Wow! Signal Emitted from 3I/ATLAS?' (Medium, Sept 28, 2025) Loeb speculated the 1977 signal could have been radio emission from 3I/ATLAS itself, calculating that a transmitter at its then-distance of ~600 AU would have needed 0.5-2 gigawatts — 'the output of a typical nuclear reactor on Earth.' He kept the directional coincidence as anomaly #12 of his final 22-item list (March 12, 2026): arrival 'coincident with the radio Wow! Signal to within 9 degrees, with a probability of 0.6%.'","loeb_quote":"The chance of two random directions in the sky being aligned to that level is about 0.6 percent.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'Was the Wow! Signal Emitted from 3I/ATLAS?', Medium, Sept 28, 2025","official_explanation":"Critics note 9 degrees is enormous in radio-astronomy terms — many times the Big Ear beam size. Penn State's Jason Wright (AstroWright, Nov 9, 2025) wrote '9 degrees from the Wow! Signal is a long way away!... it very definitely did not come from the direction of the Wow! Signal,' calling the anomaly 'ridiculous.' In 1977 the object was ~600 AU away with no plausible natural emission mechanism, and the Wow! Signal itself is widely attributed to terrestrial interference or a transient natural hydrogen-line source (e.g., Abel Mendez's cold hydrogen cloud hypothesis).","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/was-the-wow-signal-emitted-from-3i-atlas-d18d4f0d1f1e","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://www.newsweek.com/interstellar-object-could-be-mysterious-wow-signal-source-astronomer-10846853","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-10","title":"Non-gravitational acceleration at perihelion","date":"2025-10-31","observation":"JPL's post-perihelion orbit solution (Davide Farnocchia, Oct 30, 2025) required a radial non-gravitational acceleration with A1 ~ 1.6e-6 AU/day^2 normalized at 1 AU — equivalent to a radial acceleration of ~135 km/day^2 (~9e-7 AU/day^2) at the 1.36 AU perihelion distance, plus a transverse component of ~60 km/day^2. As the data arc grew the coefficient dropped: ~4e-7 AU/day^2 by Nov 24, then ~6.8e-8 AU/day^2 under a 1/r^2 (CO2-sublimation) model. In 2026 Loeb continued the thread, arguing (Mar 2026) the acceleration is not directed purely away from the Sun and linking it (Apr 2026) to the object's three-jet system.","loeb_take":"In 'First Evidence for a Non-Gravitational Acceleration of 3I/ATLAS at Perihelion' and 'Afterthoughts...' (both Oct 31, 2025), Loeb calculated that producing the initial acceleration by rocket-effect outgassing would require 3I/ATLAS to shed on the order of a tenth to a sixth of its mass near perihelion (his figure quoted as ~13% in critiques) — at least 5.5 billion tons from a >=33-billion-ton nucleus — which should have produced a massive observable gas cloud by December. Absent that cloud, he argued, the acceleration 'might be regarded as a technological signature' of an internal propulsion system.","loeb_quote":"If we do not observe a massive cloud of gas around 3I/ATLAS in December, then the reported non-gravitational acceleration near perihelion might be regarded as a technological signature.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/afterthoughts-on-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-at-perihelion-97c609e24fc6","official_explanation":"Non-gravitational accelerations are standard comet behavior; the measured value shrank more than 20-fold as more astrometry accumulated, and the final magnitude is consistent with ordinary volatile outgassing (arXiv:2511.07450 finds CO-driven outgassing reproduces it with no exotic volatiles needed). Early solutions were inflated by systematic astrometric biases — ~0.5 arcsec tailward coma-photocenter offsets and the October solar-conjunction data gap (arXiv:2603.00782) — and Jason Wright noted that even 13% mass loss near perihelion 'is hardly weird' for a comet.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"acceleration","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/first-evidence-for-a-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-at-perihelion-2698f6a453fe","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/afterthoughts-on-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-at-perihelion-97c609e24fc6","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/updates-on-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-89ee7220c5d7","https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.07450"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-11","title":"Anomalous brightening and blue color at perihelion","date":"2025-10-30","observation":"Analysis of STEREO-A (SECCHI HI1/COR2), SOHO/LASCO C3 and GOES-19 CCOR-1 coronagraph imagery (Zhang, Lowell Observatory & Battams, NRL; arXiv:2510.25035, submitted Oct 28, 2025) showed 3I/ATLAS brightening near perihelion roughly as r^-7.5 (±1.0) — far steeper than typical comets (r^-2 to r^-4) — while its color turned bluer than the Sun, unusual because dust comae normally redden reflected sunlight.","loeb_take":"Loeb flagged it on Oct 30, 2025 as the ninth anomaly on his running list, asking whether the object 'employs a power source hotter than the Sun'. In his Nov 23, 2025 likelihood ranking he placed it in his least-anomalous category at P<0.1: it 'brightened faster than any known comet' while bluer than sunlight, which he suggested might be 'a signature that its engine turned on' near perihelion rather than sunlight-driven sublimation. It appears as item #22 on his final 22-anomaly list (March 12, 2026).","loeb_quote":"Near perihelion, 3I/ATLAS brightened faster than any known comet and was bluer than the Sun","quote_source":"'3I/ATLAS is Fading Away, Leaving Us to Ponder Over Its 22 Mysterious Anomalies', Medium, March 12, 2026 (same wording in 'Anomalies of 3I/ATLAS, Organized by Likelihood', Nov 23, 2025)","official_explanation":"A gas-dominated coma explains both facts: rapid CO2/CO/H2O outgassing close to the Sun drives steep brightening, and gas emission bands (CN, C2, CO+) are intrinsically blue, dominating over reddish dust in broadband coronagraph photometry. Zhang & Battams themselves conclude the blue color is consistent with gas emission contributing a substantial fraction of the visible brightness. Fast-moving interstellar comets get less heating time inbound, so late, sharp activity onsets are expected.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.25035","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-rapidly-brightens-and-gets-bluer-than-the-sun-near-perihelion-3bf100df8390","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","https://skyandtelescope.org/astronomy-news/updates-on-interstellar-comet-3i-atlas-new-images-many-tails-and-non-gravitational-forces/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-12","title":"Extreme mass combined with extreme speed","date":"2025-11-23","observation":"From the smallness of the non-gravitational acceleration (<15 m/day^2, from 4,022 MPC astrometric observations May 15-Sep 23, 2025) given Webb-measured outgassing of ~150 kg/s, Loeb and colleagues derived (arXiv 2509.21408, Sep 25, 2025) a minimum nucleus mass of ~33 billion tons and minimum diameter ~5 km — roughly a million times 'Oumuamua and a thousand times Borisov — while 3I/ATLAS also moves faster than both (hyperbolic excess speed ~58 km/s vs 26 km/s for 'Oumuamua and 32 km/s for Borisov). After perihelion, Loeb revised the mass estimate down to ~1 billion tons (~100,000x 'Oumuamua) once a small non-gravitational acceleration was detected, but kept the mass-speed pairing on his anomaly lists.","loeb_take":"In his Nov 23, 2025 likelihood-ranked list Loeb assigned this P<0.001 (a Category I 'major' anomaly): known reservoirs of interstellar material should not regularly deliver objects ~a million times more massive than 'Oumuamua, and the mass-speed combination compounds the statistical strain. It appears as anomaly #20 of his final 22 (March 12, 2026). The large nucleus mass also feeds his separate 'mass-budget discrepancy' argument (anomaly #1 of the 22): the implied population of 3I-like objects would exceed the heavy-element mass available in interstellar space.","loeb_quote":"The nucleus of 3I/ATLAS is roughly a million times more massive than 1I/`Oumuamua and a thousand times more massive than 2I/Borisov, while moving faster than both","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","official_explanation":"Jason Wright (AstroWright, Nov 9, 2025) shows the mass floor rests on claiming a non-gravitational-acceleration precision of 1e-10 AU/day^2 when the real precision is ~1e-7 — a thousand-fold error — and notes Loeb's own non-gravitational-acceleration anomaly uses data at the coarser precision ('he can't have it both ways'); mainstream size estimates keep the nucleus km-scale (Hubble upper limit ~2.8 km diameter). The higher speed is separately explained by mainstream kinematics: 3I/ATLAS's velocity is consistent with an origin in the Galaxy's older, dynamically hotter thick-disk population, implying a kinematic age of ~7-10 Gyr — consistent with later isotope evidence of great age.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/anomalies-of-3i-atlas-organized-by-likelihood-af20fb3b6d21","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.21408","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-13","title":"Perijove distance equals Jupiter's Hill radius","date":"2025-11-22","observation":"The predicted minimum distance from Jupiter on Mar 16, 2026 — 53.445 +/- 0.06 million km — matched Jupiter's Hill radius (53.502 million km) to within one standard deviation, about one part in a thousand. Loeb put the probability of the coincidence at less than 0.00004 (about 1 in 26,000, scaled to Jupiter's orbital diameter) and noted the non-gravitational acceleration measured near perihelion shifted the perijove by ~0.1 million km — exactly the course correction needed to produce the match.","loeb_take":"In 'An Extraordinary New Anomaly of 3I/ATLAS' (Nov 22, 2025), Loeb argued a passage precisely at the Hill radius — where Jupiter's Lagrange points L1 and L2 sit — is where an artificial object could release technological devices into bound Jovian orbits with minimal fuel and orbital corrections, and suggested Juno or other human-made orbiters at Jupiter could search for new objects orbiting the planet.","loeb_quote":"the level of non-gravitational acceleration was finely tuned","quote_source":"'An Extraordinary New Anomaly of 3I/ATLAS', Medium, November 22, 2025","official_explanation":"Astronomers called the statistics post-hoc numerology: astrophysicists including Scott Manley and David Kipping publicly flagged flaws in Loeb's probability analysis, and critics (e.g. Steve Desch, Jason Wright) noted such 'fine-tuning' probabilities are computed after the fact from one of many possible 'special' distances. The prediction itself dissolved: the actual March 16, 2026 pass occurred at 53.6 million km (0.358330 AU) — slightly outside the Hill radius — with ESA's JUICE observing an ordinary cometary coma with dust and ion tails, and no new satellites of Jupiter were reported.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-remarkable-new-anomaly-of-3i-atlas-420065c2cddf","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/q-a-about-3i-atlas-a-week-before-its-closest-approach-to-jupiter-2d41a2bcccfd","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3I/ATLAS","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-14","title":"Dust particle size paradox","date":"2025-12-25","observation":"Sustaining the ~400,000 km sunward anti-tail jet requires dust grains far larger than the sub-micron particles that dominate ordinary comet comae: Loeb brackets them between ~1 and ~100 microns (characteristic radius ~10 microns) — big enough to resist solar radiation pressure, small enough for gas drag to accelerate to jet speeds. Millimeter-scale or larger grains are excluded because the total ejected dust mass needed to produce the observed scattered sunlight would become implausibly large for the nucleus.","loeb_take":"Loeb kept this as anomaly #16 of his final 22-item list (Mar 12, 2026): the glow is dominated by particles much larger than common sub-micron interstellar dust, yet they cannot exceed millimeter size without mass-budget inconsistencies — an anomalous size bracket for a comet. From ~10-micron grains he estimated ~10 million kg of dust shed (~3.3 kg/s) and asked whether 3I/ATLAS accumulated such grains in a molecular cloud.","loeb_quote":"3I/ATLAS is therefore anomalous in the size of the particles that dominate the glow of its sunward jet.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-sheds-particles-that-are-much-bigger-than-common-sunlight-scattering-dust-703cc2220df0","official_explanation":"Refereed HST analysis (Jewitt & Luu 2025, ApJL) self-consistently explains the sunward fan that evolved into an anti-sunward tail as large grains (~100 microns) ejected slowly (~5 m/s) — behavior well documented in low-activity and distantly active comets — and an MNRAS study of the physics of cometary anti-tails as observed in 3I/ATLAS reaches similar conclusions. Mass-loss estimates fit cometary norms once realistic size distributions (not single-size grains) are used; grain properties vary widely between comets and no paradox is recognized in the refereed literature.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-sheds-particles-that-are-much-bigger-than-common-sunlight-scattering-dust-703cc2220df0","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-dust-mass-shed-by-3i-atlas-b4ed260addef","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/adf8d8"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-15","title":"Rotation axis aligned with sunward direction","date":"2025-12-16","observation":"A wobbling high-latitude jet observed pre-perihelion (Serra-Ricart, Licandro & Alarcon, arXiv 2512.12819) yielded a spin-axis solution (sky-projected PA 280.7 deg, ~15.48 h rotation period). Loeb inferred from this geometry that the nucleus spin axis was aligned to within 8 degrees of the sunward direction as 3I/ATLAS entered the solar system, and put the probability of a random spin pole landing there at 0.5% (P=0.005). An earlier Loeb & Scarmato coma-morphology analysis had found a looser 10-20 degree alignment (1.5-6% probability).","loeb_take":"First flagged Dec 16, 2025 as the '14th anomaly'; it became anomaly #6 of the final 22. Loeb argued a sun-pointing spin axis gives the object a permanent dayside and nightside, and that if the antipodal jet geometry requires a second sublimating ice pocket placed near the opposite pole with similar precision, the improbability compounds multiplicatively to 0.005 x 0.005 = 2.5e-5 - fine-tuned orientation he suggests is more naturally read as a maintained attitude than a random tumble.","loeb_quote":"The probability for that is 0.5%.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"Spin-pole solutions derived from fuzzy coma and jet features are highly model-dependent (Loeb's own earlier analysis gave 10-20 degrees, and independent coma-shape fits allowed two different poles), and seasonal activity biases naturally select for apparent sunward alignment since sun-facing active regions dominate observed morphology. A published natural mechanism also exists: torque from asymmetric ice sublimation can drive the spin axis toward the sunward direction, and a ~90-degree obliquity nucleus with a sun-pointed pole naturally explains dayside-dominant sublimation - no design implication required.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-14th-anomaly-of-3i-atlas-alignment-of-its-rotation-axis-with-the-sunward-direction-at-large-e8a08a0705d4","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.12819"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-16","title":"Wobbling high-latitude sunward jet","date":"2025-12-16","observation":"A pre-perihelion study (arXiv:2512.12819, submitted 2025-12-14; Serra-Ricart, Licandro & Alarcon, A&A 705, L3) detected a faint high-latitude jet in the inner coma at PA 280±10 degrees across seven August 2025 nights, with a periodic 7.74±0.35 h modulation consistent with a high-latitude jet precessing around the nucleus spin axis (implied rotation period 15.48±0.70 h). Loeb argued the sunward wobble requires the jet's base to lie within 8 degrees of the sun-facing rotation pole, and assigned that pole-proximate location a probability of 0.5%.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #7 on his 22-anomaly list: the jet's base sits improbably close to the sun-facing pole (0.5%). Combined with the matching post-perihelion coincidence at the opposite pole (anomaly #8), he squares it: 0.5% x 0.5% = 0.000025 (2.5e-5) that sunward anti-tail jets appear both before and after perihelion by chance.","loeb_quote":"The observed wobble of the pre-perihelion anti-tail jet in the direction of the Sun (as reported here during July and August 2025) requires the base of the jet to be within 8 degrees from the sun-facing pole, with a probability of 0.5%.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"The discovery paper itself treats this as ordinary comet physics: high-latitude vents that wobble with rotation are documented in many comets (e.g., 67P, Hale-Bopp), and a near-polar active region is exactly what sustained sun-facing illumination would activate. The 'independent' probabilities Loeb multiplies are not independent — they describe the same geometry.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.12819","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-anti-tail-of-3i-atlas-wobbled-before-perihelion-3a0d286a6ad3","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-17","title":"Identical collimated jets before and after perihelion","date":"2025-12-17","observation":"After perihelion, 3I/ATLAS re-established a collimated sunward anti-tail jet with essentially the same ~8 degree opening angle and orientation as before perihelion, extending to ~1 million km in Dec 15, 2025 imaging — despite the intervening 16-degree (16.4 deg) gravitational deflection of its trajectory, which Loeb noted is exactly twice the jet's opening angle.","loeb_take":"In his Dec 17, 2025 essay Loeb combined the pre- and post-perihelion pointing likelihoods (0.5% each) into P = 0.5%^2 = 0.000025 (2.5e-5, ~1 in 40,000) — one of the smallest single-coincidence probabilities he quoted for any 3I/ATLAS anomaly — arguing persistent precision pointing through perihelion resembles station-kept thrust. This essay is where he consolidated the count at 15 anomalies (formalized next day in 'The 15 Anomalies of 3I/ATLAS'); he kept the object at rank 4 on his Loeb Classification Scale pending public release of data bracketing the Dec 19 perigee.","loeb_quote":"The fact that a tightly collimated jet appears as the sunward anti-tail both before and after perihelion (while reversing direction at perihelion relative to the direction of motion), has a tiny probability of occurring at random, equal to the square of 0.5% or 0.000025.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","official_explanation":"A jet tied to the nucleus spin axis SHOULD look similar before and after perihelion: gravity deflects the orbit, not the spin axis, so no independent coincidence exists. Josh Winn (in a Dec 18, 2025 exchange Loeb published) objected that Loeb's statistical questions are constructed after already knowing the observations; Jason Wright added that sunward anti-tails/jets are precedented in comets (17P/Holmes, C/2016 R2, C/1961 R1, explained in the literature decades ago) and that multiplying a-posteriori probabilities is statistically invalid.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-15-anomalies-of-3i-atlas-should-we-pay-attention-to-them-if-they-were-not-forecasted-77375f9974d5","https://www.ibtimes.co.uk/avi-loeb-keeps-3i-atlas-rank-4-suspense-builds-new-data-looms-1764870"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-18","title":"Jet base 'insulation' on the nightside","date":"2025-12-17","observation":"Because 3I/ATLAS's rotation axis is nearly aligned with the sunward direction, each anti-tail jet's launch base sits on the nightside for months at a stretch (the post-perihelion jet's base was on the nightside before perihelion, and vice versa), yet the jets are active only while their base faces the Sun.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #9 of Loeb's 22-item wrap-up list (March 2026; first argued as point #8 in his Dec 17, 2025 essay): for the bases to stay dormant through months of darkness they must be extremely well insulated, but heat should naturally conduct throughout a comet's body — so, per Loeb, the on/off pattern hints at engineered surfaces or controlled venting rather than passive ice.","loeb_quote":"For these bases to be active only when facing the Sun, they must be well insulated on the nightside for a period longer than several months. However, heat would naturally flow by conduction throughout the body of a natural comet, making this insulation requirement difficult to satisfy.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, '3I/ATLAS Maintained a Sunward Jet After Its Gravitational Deflection by 16 Degrees at Perihelion', Medium, Dec 17, 2025 (repeated as anomaly #9 in the Mar 12, 2026 22-anomaly list)","official_explanation":"Dayside-dominant activity is textbook comet behavior, and real cometary crusts are naturally superb insulators: Rosetta's MIRO/VIRTIS instruments measured 67P's thermal inertia at roughly 10-60 J m^-2 K^-1 s^-1/2 — a powdery, thermally insulating surface where solar heat penetrates only centimeters. Heat conduction through a porous nucleus is extremely inefficient, contradicting the premise that warmth should spread to the nightside; no exotic insulation is required. No agency-level rebuttal addresses this numbered claim specifically, but the underlying thermophysics is standard.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2018/08/aa33104-18/aa33104-18.html"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-19","title":"Gravitational deflection exactly twice the jet opening angle","date":"2025-12-17","observation":"In a Dec 17, 2025 essay, Loeb calculated that solar gravity bent 3I/ATLAS's velocity vector by ~16 degrees (2GM/(b*v^2) = 0.286 rad = 16.4 deg) at perihelion, and noted this is almost exactly twice the ~8-degree opening angle of the narrow sunward anti-tail jet measured in pre-perihelion imaging; Hubble images from Dec 12 and 27, 2025 showed the sunward jet persisting after the deflection.","loeb_take":"Loeb flagged the 2:1 ratio as one more geometric coincidence in his anomaly ledger — it became anomaly #10 of the final 22 (Geometric Coincidences) in his March 12, 2026 wrap-up essay. He also used the geometry constructively: a jet wobbling within an ~8-degree cone about a fixed spin axis could produce the sunward anti-tail before perihelion and a counter-jet after it.","loeb_quote":"The gravitational deflection of 3I/ATLAS by 16 degrees at perihelion, is exactly twice the opening angle of the anti-tail jet.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","official_explanation":"No mainstream astronomer published a rebuttal to this specific angle ratio. The general response to Loeb's coincidence claims (e.g., Jason Wright's critiques of his fine-tuning statistics) applies: the two angles arise from unrelated physics — the 16-degree deflection is routine orbital mechanics for any body with that perihelion distance and speed, while the jet opening angle is a soft, method-dependent measurement — and comparing enough angle pairs post hoc guarantees some near-integer ratios.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-maintained-a-sunward-jet-after-its-gravitational-deflection-by-16-degrees-at-perihelion-e6810be9b3d8","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://sites.psu.edu/astrowright/2025/11/09/loebs-3i-atlas-anomalies-explained/"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-20","title":"SPHEREx icy-grain signature disappearance","date":"2026-01-11","observation":"SPHEREx detected strong water-ice absorption in the coma pre-perihelion (Aug 2025, arXiv:2508.15469), but on re-observation in December 2025 the H2O-ice spectral signature had mostly disappeared while activity rose sharply — H2O gas emission ~40x brighter — and new coma species appeared (CN, organic C-H, CO2, CO) along with refractory organo-silicaceous dust (arXiv:2601.06759, Lisse et al., RNAAS, posted 2026-01-11).","loeb_take":"Loeb listed the vanishing ice signature plus newly appearing gas-phase organics (CH3OH, H2CO, CH4, C2H6) and a ~20x jump in water production as anomaly #17 of the 22-anomaly list in his March 2026 'fading away' essay. [paraphrase] He argued the transformation was not predicted in advance, and that organics surviving cosmic-ray bombardment over an interstellar journey would require burial under at least 10 meters of material.","loeb_quote":"the spectral signature of ice disappeared in the SPHEREx data after perihelion","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, Medium, '3I/ATLAS is Fading Away, Leaving Us to Ponder Over Its 22 Mysterious Anomalies' (March 2026)","official_explanation":"The SPHEREx team describes expected post-perihelion evolution: solar heating sublimated the icy grains (removing the ice absorption signature) and drove the nucleus into much higher activity, releasing additional gas species. The paper's abstract describes marked evidence for development into a cometary body fully sublimating all its ices; press coverage called it a 'full cometary awakening'.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.06759","https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15469","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://phys.org/news/2026-01-spherex-imaging-reveals-sublimation-3iatlas.html"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-21","title":"Rare Sun-Earth axis alignment (Jan 22, 2026)","date":"2026-01-13","observation":"On Jan 22, 2026, 3I/ATLAS crossed the Sun-Earth axis at a phase angle of just 0.69 degrees (near-perfect opposition), with the phase angle below 2 degrees for about a week (Jan 19-26) and the anti-tail direction projected toward Earth at the moment of alignment.","loeb_take":"Loeb (with Mauro Barbieri, arXiv:2601.08624) announced the alignment on Jan 13, 2026 primarily as a 'narrow but well-defined observational window' to measure the opposition surge and probe whether the dust is pristine (fluffy fractal aggregates) or thermally processed; in his final March 2026 recap he then catalogued it as anomaly #11 of 22 — the alignment with the anti-tail pointing at Earth joining his chain of improbable geometric coincidences.","loeb_quote":"The unique near-opposition geometry of 3I/ATLAS on 22 January, 2026 provides a narrow but well-defined observational window.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-rare-alignment-of-3i-atlas-with-the-sun-earth-axis-on-22-january-2026-b1bea4a553f7","official_explanation":"Opposition alignments are a function of Earth's orbital motion, not of the object: every outer body passes near the Sun-Earth axis roughly once per synodic period (what was unusual here was only the very small 0.69-degree minimum phase angle). Mainstream astronomers used the same window productively — Hubble imaged 3I/ATLAS during the alignment, and coordinated photometry measured an opposition surge of about 0.2 mag with ~3-degree width and a linear phase slope of ~0.026 mag/deg, values consistent with ordinary cometary dust. Calling Earth's own viewing geometry an anomaly of the object is a category error.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"timing","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/a-rare-alignment-of-3i-atlas-with-the-sun-earth-axis-on-22-january-2026-b1bea4a553f7","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08624","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/hubble-images-of-3i-atlas-during-its-rare-alignment-with-the-sun-earth-axis-on-january-22-2026-7ee2c20a70f0"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-22","title":"Methane volatility puzzle","date":"2026-01-29","observation":"Methane (CH4) was first detected in the coma only after perihelion — JWST/MIRI spectra on 2025 Dec 15-16 and 27 (2.20 and 2.54 au outbound) measured CH4 at 13.7% and 27% of the water production rate — despite CH4's volatility lying between CO2 and CO, both of which were detected well before perihelion (JWST/SPHEREx, Aug 2025, saw strong CO2 and CO but no CH4). Under simple sublimation ordering, CH4 should have appeared alongside them inbound.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #19 of his final 22-anomaly list (the 'Possible Bio-Signature' item): the out-of-order appearance of volatiles violates the layered-ice sublimation sequence expected for a natural comet, suggesting to Loeb that the plume chemistry may not be simple ice sublimation — he floated abiotic (deep-buried CH4) and speculative biological interpretations.","loeb_quote":"The volatility of methane (CH4) is between that of carbon dioxide (CO2) and carbon monoxide (CO), yet methane was detected in the gas plume around 3I/ATLAS only after perihelion, while CO2 and CO were detected long before perihelion.","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"Comet nuclei are heterogeneous: the JWST/MIRI team (Belyakov et al. 2026) attributes the late CH4 onset to past depletion of methane from the outermost processed layers, with the observed CH4 emerging from relatively unprocessed subsurface material once perihelion heating reached deeper layers — or to compositional heterogeneity of the nucleus combined with seasonal illumination effects. Volatile release order varies among well-studied solar-system comets, so a late CH4 onset is within natural diversity.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22034","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-massive-nucleus-of-3i-atlas-and-its-puzzling-methane-outgassing-based-on-new-data-from-the-72e540d8fe46","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-23","title":"Three symmetric mini-jets at 120 degrees","date":"2026-01-19","observation":"Larson-Sekanina rotational-gradient filtering of Hubble Space Telescope images (key frames 2026-01-14; pattern persistent in frames from Nov 30, Dec 4, 12, 27, 2025 and Jan 7, 2026) removed the circular coma glow and revealed three mini-jets around the nucleus separated from each other by approximately 120 degrees in sky projection, plus a fourth prominent sunward anti-tail jet extending about 10 times farther. Loeb reported the jet structure wobbles by +/-20 degrees with a 7.1-hour period.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #13 of the final 22: Loeb highlighted the engineered look of three jets 'symmetrically separated by 120 degrees' from each other, noting none points anti-sunward as a normal tail would, and on 2026-04-16 published an essay (with Toni Scarmato) linking this three-jet system to the measured non-gravitational acceleration via rocket-effect thrust — discussing whether the jets are 'technological thrusters or pockets of ice' and citing a proposed 3+1-thruster configuration as an optimal steering architecture.","loeb_quote":"The 3 mini-jets are separated by 120 degrees from each other in sky projection","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/are-the-three-mini-jets-coming-out-of-3i-atlas-at-120-degree-separation-a-technological-signature-ba748a017aa0","official_explanation":"Multiple discrete vents are common on comet nuclei (Rosetta imaged dozens on 67P), and apparent regular spacing is a projection and small-number effect: with three jets, some near-symmetric arrangement is unremarkable. Jet-driven torques and acceleration from asymmetric outgassing are standard comet dynamics.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"tail","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/are-the-three-mini-jets-coming-out-of-3i-atlas-at-120-degree-separation-a-technological-signature-ba748a017aa0","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/linking-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-to-its-symmetric-system-of-three-jets-847859455a16","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-24","title":"Sideways (non-radial) non-gravitational acceleration","date":"2026-03-03","observation":"A February 2026 reanalysis of the orbit fits (Spada, Krolikowska & Dones, arXiv:2603.00782) showed the non-gravitational acceleration was not purely directed away from the Sun (as rocket-effect sublimation predicts to first order): Loeb highlighted that the tangential component was comparable in magnitude to the radial one, whereas NASA/JPL's fit had the radial component about 5x the tangential — itself well above the ~0.03-0.05 transverse-to-radial ratio typical of solar-system comets.","loeb_take":"Anomaly #14 of his 22-anomaly list (March 12, 2026): a sideways push is hard to get from sun-driven sublimation but natural for directed thrust; in his April 2026 analysis with Toni Scarmato (arXiv:2604.18773) Loeb connected it to the symmetric three-jet system, noting the jets could be 'technological thrusters or pockets of ice' — i.e., a candidate steering mechanism.","loeb_quote":"The non-gravitational acceleration of 3I/ATLAS was not directed away from the Sun but had a substantial sideways component","quote_source":"https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","official_explanation":"Standard cometary NGA models (Marsden A1/A2/A3) have always included transverse and normal components, produced by rotation-lagged outgassing — sublimation peaks in the comet 'afternoon', displacing thrust from the sunward line. The mainstream rebuttal (Spada et al. 2026) is that the fitted transverse component A2 is the least stable parameter — highly sensitive to data selection, parameter correlations, and orbital phase coverage — while total acceleration estimates agree with JPL's solution and require no exotic physics.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"acceleration","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-is-not-directed-away-from-the-sun-f478b82f7175","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/linking-the-non-gravitational-acceleration-of-3i-atlas-to-its-symmetric-system-of-three-jets-847859455a16","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/3i-atlas-is-fading-away-leaving-us-to-ponder-over-its-22-mysterious-anomalies-8d1318565f13","https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00782"]},{"object":"3i","id":"A-25","title":"Anomalous isotope abundances (D/H, 12C/13C, 14N/15N)","date":"2026-03-10","observation":"JWST (Cordiner et al.) reported deuterium-enriched water with D/H more than ten times higher than known comets, and 12C/13C of 141-191 (CO2) and 123-172 (CO), above typical solar-system values; VLT (Opitom et al.) found 12C/13C = 147 (+87/-40) and 14N/15N = 343 (+454/-124), over twice the ~150 typical of solar-system comets.","loeb_take":"In 'New Anomalies in the Isotope Abundances of 3I/ATLAS' (Mar 10, 2026) Loeb accepted the papers' inference of formation below 30 K in a metal-poor environment 10-12 billion years ago, but argued that ancient low-metallicity stellar populations cannot supply enough rocky material — they 'miss the required mass budget by at least three orders of magnitude'. The same day he reduced 3I/ATLAS to rank 3 on the Loeb scale, conditional on an uneventful Jupiter pass.","loeb_quote":"miss the required mass budget by at least three orders of magnitude","quote_source":"'New Anomalies in the Isotope Abundances of 3I/ATLAS', Medium, March 10, 2026","official_explanation":"The measuring teams interpret the isotopes as strong evidence for a natural, extremely ancient comet formed at freezing temperatures early in the Milky Way's history — the isotopic signature of great age, not technology. The mass-budget objection depends on Loeb's contested large-mass estimate; for a km-scale nucleus no reservoir problem arises.","loeb_scale":3,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://avi-loeb.medium.com/new-anomalies-in-the-isotope-abundances-of-3i-atlas-1bb3790207f1","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/q-a-about-3i-atlas-a-week-before-its-closest-approach-to-jupiter-2d41a2bcccfd"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-01","title":"Origin at the Local Standard of Rest (the 'buoy at rest')","date":"2017-10-31","observation":"Before entering the solar system, 'Oumuamua was moving at only ~10 km/s relative to the Local Standard of Rest (LSR): its heliocentric velocity was (U,V,W) = (-11.5, -22.4, -7.7) km/s (26.32 km/s total), which in the LSR frame leaves negligible radial (U) and vertical (W) galactic motion and only a ~11 km/s lag in the rotational (V) component. Mamajek (arXiv:1710.11364, submitted Oct 31, 2017) computed that its velocity sat within 5 km/s of the median UVW velocity of local field stars and that fewer than ~1 in 500 field stars in the solar neighborhood have velocities so close to that local median (essentially the LSR). The Sun itself moves faster relative to the LSR (~18 km/s peculiar motion) than 'Oumuamua did.","loeb_take":"Loeb argued this is exactly where you would park an artificial object meant to survey passing stars — or to hide its origin: in 'Six Strange Facts about 'Oumuamua' (arXiv:1811.08832, Nov 2018) he noted only one star in 500 moves as slowly in the LSR frame and called the LSR 'the ideal frame for camouflage,' avoiding association with any particular star. In 'Extraterrestrial' (2021) he counted the ~1-in-500 kinematic coincidence among the improbabilities any natural ejection model must explain, since ejected rocks inherit their parent star's velocity plus an ejection kick — 'Oumuamua was effectively at rest in the galactic frame and the solar system ran into it.","loeb_quote":"Perhaps 'Oumuamua was like a buoy resting in the expanse of the universe, and our solar system was like a ship that ran into it at high speed.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'Extraterrestrial' (2021) — verbatim; same buoy-and-ship analogy repeated in 2019-2021 interviews. The LSR/1-in-500 'camouflage' argument is in 'Six Strange Facts about 'Oumuamua' (arXiv:1811.08832; Scientific American, Nov 20, 2018).","official_explanation":"Young objects ejected at low velocity from nearby young stellar systems naturally cluster near the LSR before gravitational scattering ('disk heating') ages their kinematics; the low LSR velocity implies 'Oumuamua is dynamically young (<~100 Myr — Hallatt & Wiegert 2020, arXiv:1911.02473, AJ; Hsieh, Laughlin & Arce 2021 linked it to the ~30 Myr Carina moving group and a molecular-cloud origin, arXiv:2105.14670, ApJ). The ISSI team (Nature Astronomy 3, 594, 2019) treated the kinematics as pointing to a young planetesimal population, not design.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"trajectory","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.11364","https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08832","https://www.scientificamerican.com/blog/observations/6-strange-facts-about-the-interstellar-visitor-oumuamua/","https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02473"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-02","title":"Extreme lightcurve elongation (~10x brightness swings)","date":"2017-11-20","observation":"Meech et al. (Nature 552, 378; published 2017-11-20 with ESO release eso1737) reported brightness variations by a factor of ~10 (about 2.5 magnitudes) every ~7.3 hours as the object rotated. The original fit implied an axis ratio of up to ~10:1; later reanalyses accounting for phase-angle effects put the most likely aspect ratio at ~6:1 (6±1) — still more elongated than any of the hundreds of thousands of asteroids and comets ever measured in the solar system (typical bodies are ~2:1 or 3:1).","loeb_take":"Loeb repeatedly listed the record-setting elongation as strange fact number one: no known solar-system body comes close, and an extreme aspect ratio is what an artificial thin structure (cigar or disc seen edge-on) would produce. In 'Six Strange Facts' (arXiv:1811.08832, also published in Scientific American on 2018-11-20) he noted the shape inference, combined with the later acceleration anomaly, pushed him toward a thin-membrane geometry rather than a rocky ellipsoid.","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Natural shaping mechanisms proposed: tidal fragmentation of a body during a close stellar or planetary encounter can spin off highly elongated splinters (Zhang & Lin, Nature Astronomy, April 2020, reproduced ~10:1 and even more elongated fragments); prolonged interstellar-dust erosion or collisional grinding can also elongate small bodies. The ISSI team (Nature Astronomy 3, 594, 2019) called the shape unusual but consistent with a purely natural origin, noting that lightcurve amplitude is enhanced at the observed phase angles (19-27 deg), softening the required ratio to ~6:1; McNeill et al. (2018) independently derived 6±1 from lightcurve inversion, and Vazan & Sari (MNRAS 493, 1546, 2020) argued irregular surface reflection properties could mimic part of the amplitude, allowing an even less elongated body.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/nature25020","https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso1737/","https://arxiv.org/pdf/1811.08832","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-019-0816-x"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-03","title":"Complex tumbling (non-principal-axis rotation)","date":"2017-12-01","observation":"Photometry from multiple telescopes could not be phased to a single rotation period; Fraser et al. (Nature Astronomy, arXiv 2017-11-30, published 2018-02-09) and Drahus et al. (Nature Astronomy 2018, Gemini North data) showed 'Oumuamua is tumbling — rotating in an excited non-principal-axis state with lightcurve periodicities near ~7.3-8.7 h (Drahus: ~7.34 h average; Belton: 8.67 h). Belton et al. (ApJL 856, L21, April 2018) formalized the spin-state families from 818 observations over 29.3 days. Fraser et al. computed a damping timescale for the tumbling of at least a billion years, so it likely tumbled through interstellar space since a violent event (e.g. a collision) long before reaching us.","loeb_take":"Loeb counted the excited spin as another oddity constraining natural origins (a collision or fragmentation is needed to set it tumbling) and argued the tumbling made the outgassing explanation for the acceleration harder: citing Rafikov (2018), he noted that cometary jets should have torqued the object and rapidly changed its spin — Rafikov showed outgassing strong enough to supply the acceleration would drive spin evolution within days (even rotational breakup) unless the jet's torque lever arm was finely tuned, yet no spin change or sudden kicks were observed. Separately, Loeb cited Mashchenko's (2019) lightcurve-torque modeling approvingly for its conclusion that the shape is disc-like at ~91% confidence — though Mashchenko's own best fit (a thin disc under moderate outgassing torque of comet-like amplitude) actually supports the natural-comet reading.","loeb_quote":"showing no change in spin or sudden kicks as routinely observed from localized jets","quote_source":"Loeb, 'On the Possibility of an Artificial Origin for `Oumuamua', Astrobiology review (lweb.cfa.harvard.edu/~loeb/Loeb_Astrobiology.pdf), citing Rafikov 2018","official_explanation":"Tumbling is common among small solar-system bodies after collisions; Fraser et al. attributed it to a collision in 'Oumuamua's birth system before or during ejection. Belton et al. showed several spin states fit the data, including ones consistent with either cigar or pancake shapes. Rafikov (2018) argued the observed spin stability limits any outgassing torques — a point against the comet interpretation — but later modeling (Mashchenko 2019; Seligman et al.) showed outgassing concentrated at the substellar point can supply the acceleration while leaving the tumbling period essentially unchanged.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-018-0398-z","https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.11530","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-018-0440-1","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/aab370"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-04","title":"Breakthrough Listen radio silence","date":"2017-12-13","observation":"On 2017-12-13 (3:45-9:45 pm ET) Breakthrough Listen observed 'Oumuamua with the Green Bank Telescope across four bands (L, S, X, C; 1.1-11.6 GHz), accumulating ~90 TB of raw data over the 2-hour on-source observation plus calibration scans. No artificial narrowband signals were found. The published analysis (Enriquez et al. 2018, Res. Notes AAS 2, 9) set limits sensitive to omnidirectional transmitters with power as low as ~0.08 W — a fraction of a cellphone — at the object's distance.","loeb_take":"Loeb (who chairs Breakthrough Starshot's advisory committee) endorsed the search and treated the null result as consistent with, not fatal to, the artifact hypothesis: a defunct piece of technological debris or a passive lightsail need not transmit. In 'Extraterrestrial' he argued most artifacts we encounter would likely be dead 'space junk' of other civilizations, so radio silence carries limited evidential weight.","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"The mainstream reading is straightforward: a natural rock does not transmit, and the deep non-detection removed the most testable technosignature prediction. The SETI Institute's Allen Telescope Array had already observed it 2017-11-23 to 12-05 (Harp et al. 2019, Acta Astronautica) and independently found no signals down to ~10 W EIRP at 1 GHz (rising to ~100 W at 7 GHz), and the ISSI team's Nature Astronomy review found no compelling evidence for an artificial origin, citing the radio quiet as consistent with a natural body.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://breakthroughinitiatives.org/news/15","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2515-5172/aaa6c9","https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.02814","https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.09355"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-05","title":"Population density too high for ejected rocks","date":"2018-03-06","observation":"Scaling from Pan-STARRS's single detection, Do, Tucker & Tonry (ApJL 855, L10, published 2018-03-06) inferred a space density of 'Oumuamua-like objects of ~0.2 per AU^3 — a mass density of ~4 Earth masses of ejecta per cubic parsec, requiring each star to account for over 10^16 ejected bodies. The required planetesimal disk mass exceeds all estimates of the solar system's own planetesimal disk by an order of magnitude; Loeb noted the density is 2-8 orders of magnitude above the pre-detection prediction of Moro-Martin, Turner & Loeb (2009), and rounded the requirement to ~10^15 (a quadrillion) objects per star.","loeb_take":"Loeb ('Six Strange Facts', arXiv:1811.08832, and book 'Extraterrestrial') argued each star would need to eject ~10^15 'Oumuamua-like objects — 2-8 orders of magnitude more than he and Moro-Martin had predicted in 2009 — an implausible budget if they are random rocks. A random population of 10^15 solar sails per star is equally unreasonable, so he argued targeted trajectories 'add explanatory power': far fewer artifacts are needed if they aim for the habitable zones of stars rather than sampling all orbits randomly. Separately, Siraj & Loeb (New Astronomy 2022) ran the same mass-budget attack against the nitrogen-iceberg model, and Loeb repeatedly cited Moro-Martin's 2018-2019 finding that ejection scenarios fall short.","loeb_quote":"Each star in the Milky-Way needs to eject 10^15 such objects during its lifetime to account for the inferred population, much more than anticipated based on the Solar System.","quote_source":"Loeb, 'Six Strange Facts about 'Oumuamua', arXiv:1811.08832 (Nov 2018; also Scientific American, 2018-11-20)","official_explanation":"The ISSI team (Bannister et al., Nature Astronomy 2019) countered that with a single detected object there are no firm constraints on the size-frequency distribution, so the measured number density cannot be converted to a mass density: plausible SFDs with a few percent of mass in ~50 m fragments match the observed density with only 0.02-15 Earth masses ejected per star, making the 'higher than expected' claim unsupported. Do et al. themselves proposed Oort-cloud shedding by post-main-sequence stars as a supplementary source; Zhang & Lin (2020) and Desch & Jackson (2021) each predict abundant small fragments. Moro-Martin (2018, 2019), however, found the tension persists under many assumptions — the debate hinged on the unknown size distribution, which detections of 2I and 3I are only now starting to constrain (km-scale ISOs are far rarer, ~10^-4 to 10^-2 per AU^3).","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/aaae67","https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.02821","https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08832","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-019-0816-x"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-06","title":"Non-gravitational acceleration with no visible coma or tail","date":"2018-06-27","observation":"Micheli et al. (Nature 559, 223; published 2018-06-27) detected, at ~30-sigma significance, a non-gravitational acceleration of 'Oumuamua of 4.92 +/- 0.16 x 10^-6 m/s^2 at 1 AU, pointed radially away from the Sun and falling off roughly as 1/r^2. Yet deep imaging (VLT, HST, Gemini) and Spitzer showed no coma, no dust tail, and no detected gas — the usual engine of such accelerations in comets. Explaining it with outgassing would need ~10 kg/s of gas with a gas-to-dust ratio >100x any known comet.","loeb_take":"This is the anomaly that launched the artifact hypothesis: an excess sunward-decaying push with no visible propellant is precisely the signature of solar radiation pressure on a thin, low-mass-per-area object. Loeb called the combination of acceleration plus no coma the single most anomalous fact about 'Oumuamua, and in later essays assigned the natural-comet explanation a low likelihood given the additional fine-tuning (no spin-up torque, no infrared detection) it requires.","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Micheli et al. themselves concluded comet-like outgassing is physically viable if 'Oumuamua has unusual thermal/compositional properties (large grains, low dust). The ISSI team (Nature Astronomy 2019) endorsed volatile outgassing below detection thresholds. Dedicated exotic-ice models followed: hydrogen iceberg (Seligman & Laughlin, ApJL 896, L8, 2020), nitrogen-ice fragment (Desch & Jackson, JGR Planets, 2021), and radiolytic H2 trapped in water ice (Bergner & Seligman, Nature 615, 610, March 2023). A minority view (Katz 2019, arXiv:1904.02218) disputed the acceleration itself, but the 30-sigma detection is broadly accepted.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"acceleration","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-018-0254-4","https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.02218","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-019-0816-x","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-022-05687-w"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-07","title":"Bialy-Loeb lightsail hypothesis (the 0.3-0.9 mm sail)","date":"2018-11-12","observation":"Bialy & Loeb (ApJL 868, L1; arXiv 2018-10-26, published 2018-11-12) showed that solar radiation pressure alone reproduces the observed 1/r^2 excess acceleration (Micheli et al. 2018) if 'Oumuamua's mass-to-area ratio is ~0.1 g/cm^2 - i.e., a thin sheet only ~0.3-0.9 mm thick for normal material densities. They calculated such a thin film would survive interstellar dust/gas ablation and tidal/centrifugal (tensile) stresses over galactic distances.","loeb_take":"The paper offered two readings: a naturally produced ultra-thin object of a new class, or an artificial origin - 'a lightsail, floating in interstellar space as debris from advanced technological equipment' - with a 'more exotic' alternative that it was a deliberate probe. Loeb expanded this into 'Extraterrestrial' (Jan 2021), arguing the artifact hypothesis was the most economical explanation of all the anomalies jointly and publicly calling artificial origin a 'serious possibility'; in his August 2025 Loeb Scale paper (arXiv:2508.09167) he retroactively ranked 'Oumuamua Level 4 ('critical threshold' - technological explanation must be considered), versus Level 0 for 2I/Borisov and Level 4 for 3I/ATLAS.","loeb_quote":"Alternatively, a more exotic scenario is that 'Oumuamua may be a fully operational probe sent intentionally to Earth vicinity by an alien civilization.","quote_source":"Bialy & Loeb 2018, ApJL 868, L1 ('Could Solar Radiation Pressure Explain 'Oumuamua's Peculiar Acceleration?'), Section 5 - exact sentence verified against the published text","official_explanation":"Curran (A&A 649, L17, 2021, ''Oumuamua as a light sail: Evidence against artificial origin') showed light sails asymptotically approach a terminal velocity, so a sail with 'Oumuamua's parameters (thickness <~1 mm, mass ~4x10^4 kg) would need roughly half a billion years just to reach the Sun from its closest likely system of origin - making an artificial sail very unlikely. Zhou et al. (A&A 667, A108, 2022) added that a tumbling sail is inconsistent with the data: <1.5% probability of matching the observed lightcurve amplitude and ~0.4% probability of remaining visible in all 55 post-discovery observations. The ISSI team (Nature Astronomy, 2019) found all observations consistent with a purely natural origin, and porous fractal-aggregate models (Moro-Martin 2019; Luu, Flekkoy & Toussaint 2020) offered a natural route to lightsail-like area-to-mass ratios.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"acceleration","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/aaeda8","https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.11490","https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2021/05/aa41283-21/aa41283-21.html","https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/abs/2022/11/aa44119-22/aa44119-22.html"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-08","title":"Spitzer infrared non-detection: small and shiny","date":"2018-11-14","observation":"Spitzer stared at 'Oumuamua's position for 30.2 hours at 4.5 microns (IRAC channel 2) on 2017-11-21/22 UT and saw nothing, with a 3-sigma flux upper limit of 0.3 microJy (Trilling et al., AJ 156, 261; published 2018-11-14). This caps the effective spherical diameter at <98, <140, or <440 m (for low/middle/high thermal beaming) and forces the albedo above 0.2, 0.1, or 0.01 respectively — plus tight 3-sigma upper limits on outgassing: CO2 < 9e22 and CO < ~9e21 molecules/s.","loeb_take":"Loeb highlighted that the preferred solutions make 'Oumuamua small and at least ~10x more reflective than typical solar-system comets and asteroids — consistent with a polished metallic or icy-clean artificial surface — while the CO/CO2 limits simultaneously starve the outgassing explanation for the acceleration of its most common propellants. Smaller size also means the non-gravitational push acted on a lower-mass object, easing the radiation-pressure requirement.","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Trilling et al. proposed that if 'Oumuamua is a comet-like body, low-level post-perihelion volatile emission after its 0.256 AU perihelion passage could have deposited a fresh, bright icy mantle, explaining the high albedo; water vapor (invisible at 4.5 microns, ~6e25 molecules/s needed) could still drive the acceleration. The nitrogen-ice model (Jackson & Desch 2021) naturally yields a high albedo (~0.64, N2 frost like Pluto's surface) and a small body (~45 x 44 x 7.5 m), fitting Spitzer's limits, as does the hydrogen-iceberg model.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-3881/aae88f","https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.08072","https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasa-learns-more-about-interstellar-visitor-oumuamua/","https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2020JE006706"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-09","title":"Pancake, not cigar: the 91% oblate fit","date":"2019-06-09","observation":"Mashchenko (MNRAS 489, 3003; arXiv:1906.03696, submitted 9 June 2019) fit the full October-November 2017 lightcurve with a physical tumbling-plus-torque model and found disc-shaped (oblate) bodies reproduce the deep brightness minima with 91% likelihood, versus only 16% for cigar shapes, which additionally suffer an orientation fine-tuning problem. The best-fit body is a thin slab of ~115 x 111 x 19 m — an aspect ratio near 6:6:1 (assumed geometric albedo 0.1) — under a modest outgassing torque whose amplitude, relative to the non-gravitational acceleration, matches Solar System comets.","loeb_take":"Loeb seized on this: the popular cigar was an artist's conception, while the data statistically favor a flat pancake at 91% confidence — far closer to a sail geometry. In essays and in 'Extraterrestrial' he emphasized that a razor-thin disc is even harder to make naturally than a cigar (Mashchenko's fits even allow a millimeter-thin sheet), and — following Rafikov's 2018 argument that outgassing torques should have measurably spun the object up — that the required torque balance further strains the invisible-outgassing story. The 2025 Loeb Scale paper (arXiv:2508.09167) again cites 1I's 'extreme geometry' among the accumulated anomalies justifying its Level 4 ranking.","loeb_quote":"","quote_source":"","official_explanation":"Natural routes to oblate shapes exist: Seligman & Laughlin (ApJL 896, L8, 2020) showed sublimation mass wasting on an H2 iceberg monotonically raises the axis ratio, sculpting the body toward the observed 6:6:1 oblate form; Jackson & Desch's N2-ice fragment (JGR Planets 2021) likewise ablates to an oblate 45 x 44 x 7.5 m sliver — 'like a bar of soap', per the AGU release; and gravitational collapse of rotating pebble clouds can yield flattened planetesimals (Lorek & Johansen, arXiv:2401.05101 — a general formation result, not an Oumuamua study). By contrast, Zhang & Lin's tidal-fragmentation simulations (Nature Astronomy 2020) produce almost exclusively prolate, cigar-like fragments and argue the elongated interpretation of the lightcurve remains viable — so the mainstream view spans both shape families, with the pancake merely statistically preferred.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.03696","https://doi.org/10.1093/mnras/stz2380","https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/489/3/3003/5556541","https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07218"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-10","title":"Counter-explanation wars and Loeb's rebuttals (2019-2023)","date":"2023-03-22","observation":"Each natural model for the coma-less acceleration drew a published Loeb counter. Hydrogen iceberg (Seligman & Laughlin, ApJL 896 L8, 2020) vs Hoang & Loeb (ApJL 899 L23, 2020): H2-ice grains cannot form in dense GMCs (collisional heating sublimates mantles) and only bodies larger than ~5 km would survive the journey. Nitrogen fragment (Jackson & Desch / Desch & Jackson, JGR Planets 126, 2021) vs Siraj & Loeb (New Astronomy 92, 101730, 2022): the required exo-Pluto mass of heavy elements exceeds the total locked in all stars (95% confidence). Radiolytic H2-in-water-ice (Bergner & Seligman, Nature 615, 610, 2023-03-22) vs Hoang & Loeb (ApJL 951 L34, 2023): neglecting H2 evaporative cooling overestimated the surface temperature by a factor of ~9, cutting the thermal speed of outgassing H2 (and thus the thrust) by a factor of ~3 — too little to propel 'Oumuamua. A separate Nature Matters Arising by N. F. W. Ligterink ('Accelerating 'Oumuamua with H2 is challenging', Nature 623, E14, 2023-11-29) independently argued GCR radiolysis cannot make enough H2 in a water-ice body.","loeb_take":"Loeb's position: the succession of exotic, never-before-observed objects (hydrogen icebergs, nitrogen icebergs, 'dust bunnies', dark comets) invented to save a natural origin shows the anomaly is real, and each fails quantitatively — so an artificial thin object remains on the table. Bergner and Seligman published a formal Reply in Nature (623, E16, 2023) defending their model against Ligterink's Matters Arising (Hoang & Loeb's critique ran in ApJL, not Nature), and the mainstream treats the 2023 Nature paper as the leading closure of the case; Loeb explicitly does not.","loeb_quote":"[paraphrase] Adding hypothetical objects we have never seen — hydrogen icebergs, nitrogen icebergs — to explain the first interstellar visitor is not simpler than considering an artificial origin.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, Medium essay ''Oumuamua Was Not a Hydrogen-Water Iceberg' (2023-03-23) and EarthSky interview 'Was 'Oumuamua a comet? Avi Loeb responds' (2023)","official_explanation":"The consensus anchor remains the ISSI 'Oumuamua team (Nature Astronomy 3, 594, 2019-07-01): every observed property is consistent with a purely natural planetesimal, and 'we find no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation'. Bergner & Seligman (2023) plus the emerging 'dark comet' population (Seligman et al. 2023-2024, small solar-system bodies with non-gravitational accelerations and no comae) are cited as showing 'Oumuamua-like behavior occurs naturally.","loeb_scale":null,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab963f","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/abab0c","https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2022NewA...9201730S/abstract","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-022-05687-w"]},{"object":"1i","id":"O-11","title":"Retroactive Loeb Scale rank: Level 4","date":"2025-08-06","observation":"In 'The Loeb Scale: Astronomical Classification of Interstellar Objects' (Eldadi, Tenenbaum & Loeb, arXiv:2508.09167, submitted Aug 6, 2025; later published in the International Journal of Astrobiology as the 'Interstellar Object Significance Scale (IOSS)'), Loeb and coauthors defined a 0-10 anomaly scale extending the Torino Scale framework (levels 0-3 natural, 4-7 increasingly anomalous, 8-10 confirmed artificial) and retroactively scored the known interstellar objects: 1I/'Oumuamua = Level 4, 2I/Borisov = Level 0, 3I/ATLAS = Level 4 (at the time of writing). A follow-up, 'Quantitative Mapping of the Loeb Scale' (Trivedi & Loeb, arXiv:2509.06253, Sep 2025), formalized the scoring numerically, explicitly modeled on Sagan's quantification of the Kardashev scale.","loeb_take":"Level 4 marks 'Oumuamua as genuinely anomalous — multiple independent deviations from any known natural class (non-gravitational acceleration without a coma of gas or dust, extreme geometry, spectral properties unexplained by conventional models) — while Borisov's Level 0 shows the scale is not alien-hype: a normal comet scores zero. Loeb used the 1I-vs-2I contrast throughout 2025-2026 essays as the template for judging 3I/ATLAS, arguing anomaly-scoring should be systematic rather than dismissed case-by-case.","loeb_quote":"[paraphrase] On the Loeb scale, 'Oumuamua ranks at Level 4 — the critical threshold, anomalous enough to warrant enhanced follow-up — while Borisov, a classical comet despite its interstellar origin, ranks Level 0.","quote_source":"Eldadi, Tenenbaum & Loeb, 'The Loeb Scale' (arXiv:2508.09167) and Avi Loeb Medium essay 'The Loeb Scale: Astronomical Classification of Interstellar Objects' (Aug 6, 2025)","official_explanation":"The scale is Loeb's framework, not community consensus — though it did pass peer review (published in the International Journal of Astrobiology). Critics note the ISSI 'Oumuamua team's 2019 Nature Astronomy paper ('The Natural History of 'Oumuamua') already found no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation, and that anomaly scales with author-controlled thresholds risk baking in the author's priors. No IAU or NASA body has adopted the scale; mainstream papers continue to classify 1I as a natural (if unusual) planetesimal and 3I/ATLAS as an active comet.","loeb_scale":4,"viz_hint":"other","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.09167","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-loeb-scale-astronomical-classification-of-interstellar-objects-62b909644351","https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06253","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/quantitative-mapping-of-the-loeb-scale-6b8b93554430"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-01","title":"Extreme carbon-monoxide enrichment — the most CO-rich comet measured inside 2.5 AU","date":"2020-04-20","observation":"Two independent teams reported on 2I/Borisov's coma in the weeks after its 2019-12-08 perihelion (q = 2.0065 AU, e = 3.3565). Bodewits et al. (HST/COS ultraviolet, four post-perihelion epochs from 2019-12-11 to 2020-01-13) derived Q(CO) = 6.4–10.7 × 10^26 molecules s^-1 (equivalent to 30–50 kg s^-1) and CO/H2O of at least 173% — 'more than three times higher than previously measured for any comet in the inner (<2.5 au) solar system,' where CO/H2O mostly ranges 0.2–23% with typical values near 4%. Simultaneous Swift/UVOT + HST/COS epochs (2019-12-19/22 and 2020-01-13) gave CO/H2O = 130–155% directly. Cordiner et al. (ALMA, 2019-12-15/16) measured Q(CO) = (4.4 ± 0.7) × 10^26 s^-1 and Q(HCN) = (7.0 ± 1.1) × 10^23 s^-1, giving CO/H2O ≈ 35–105%, HCN/H2O ≈ 0.06–0.16% (ordinary), and CO/HCN = 630 (+200/−340) — higher than any comet previously observed except the peculiar Oort Cloud comet C/2016 R2 (PanSTARRS), whose CO/HCN was 26,400 at rH = 2.8 AU. Excluding C/2016 R2, the next-highest CO/HCN was 238 ± 21 for C/2006 W3 (Christensen) at rH = 3.3 AU. The ALMA/NASA release put Borisov's CO abundance at 9–26× the solar-system comet average.","loeb_take":"Loeb has never treated Borisov's CO excess as a technosignature — it is chemistry, not engineering. In his retrospective framing Borisov is the one interstellar object that looked ordinary: a comet whose volatiles simply record a colder, more carbon-rich birth disk than the Sun's. In the 2025 Loeb Scale paper he co-authored (formally the Interstellar Object Significance Scale, IOSS; Eldadi, Tenenbaum & Loeb, arXiv:2508.09167, submitted 2025-08-06) Borisov is assigned Level 0 — fully consistent with natural processes — the lowest score of the three interstellar objects, against Level 4 for both 1I/'Oumuamua and 3I/ATLAS.","loeb_quote":"Among the four interstellar objects, only Borisov appeared familiar. It was a comet, not much different from the analogous snowballs found in the Solar system.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'The Unfamiliar Nature of Interstellar Objects,' Medium, 2022-09-23 (verified verbatim)","official_explanation":"Both teams read the CO excess as a formation-environment signature, not an anomaly. Cordiner et al.: Borisov 'must have formed in a relatively CO-rich environment — probably beyond the CO ice-line in the very cold, outer regions of a distant protoplanetary accretion disk, as part of a population of small, icy bodies analogous to our Solar System's own proto-Kuiper Belt.' Bodewits et al. add that the volatile carbon-to-oxygen ratio is 'nearly six times higher than that of previously measured solar system comets,' and derive three constraints on the host system: it must be chemically distinct from ours, Borisov must have formed beyond a CO snowline (with CO ice condensing alongside H2O rather than trapped in clathrate), and the system must have undergone dynamical stripping of planetesimals from beyond that snowline — an M-dwarf host, whose CO snowline sits near 6.6 AU, fits well. Bodewits et al. call the result 'the first glimpse into the ice content and chemical composition of the protoplanetary disk of another star that is substantially different from our own.' Notably, the papers do NOT treat known CO-rich solar-system comets as equivalent cases: Bodewits et al. explicitly exclude C/2016 R2 (PanSTARRS) from the comparison because its 2.6 AU perihelion meant it 'never reached the inner solar system where water sublimation is fully effective,' no H2O was detected in its coma, and its dominant volatile is most likely CO2 rather than CO. CO-driven comets outside 2.5 AU do commonly show CO exceeding H2O, so a CO-rich nucleus is not unprecedented in kind — but Borisov's ratio inside 2.5 AU, where water sublimation is fully effective, is the record.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.08972","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-020-1095-2","https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.09586","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-020-1087-2"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-02","title":"Atomic nickel (and iron) vapor in a 180 K coma — and the solar-system comets that did the same thing","date":"2021-05-19","observation":"Guzik & Drahus (Nature 593, 375–378) detected gaseous atomic Ni I in Borisov's cold coma from VLT/X-shooter spectra taken 28, 30 and 31 January 2020 UT at a mean heliocentric distance of 2.322 AU (geocentric 2.064 AU) — equilibrium temperature ~180 K, far below the >700 K normally needed to vaporize metal-bearing dust. Production rate Q(Ni) = (0.9 ± 0.3) × 10^22 atoms s^-1, i.e. 0.002% of OH and 0.3% of CN, from a short-lived nickel-bearing parent with a 340 (+260/-200) s lifetime at 1 AU. Opitom et al. 2021 (A&A 650, L19; VLT/UVES, 12 epochs over ~21 h, 2019-11-15 to 2020-03-16, spanning 2.1 AU pre-perihelion to 2.6 AU post-perihelion) independently found Fe I and Ni I, measuring log(Ni I/Fe I) = 0.21 ± 0.18 for 2I against an average of -0.06 ± 0.31 for 17 solar-system comets — i.e. an ordinary ratio.","loeb_take":"Loeb explicitly uses Borisov as the metals baseline rather than an anomaly: he notes Borisov's Ni/Fe ratio matched ordinary comets, which is exactly why he treats 3I/ATLAS's early nickel-heavy, iron-poor plume as the outlier worth flagging. Under his own scale Borisov's metal chemistry contributes nothing to its Level 0 score.","loeb_quote":"The interstellar comet 2I/Borisov showed nickel and iron with an abundance ratio that is similar to that observed in solar system comets.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'New Data on the Extreme Nickel Production of 3I/ATLAS,' Medium, 2025-10-01","official_explanation":"Published back-to-back in the same issue of Nature, Manfroid, Hutsemékers & Jehin (593, 372–374) showed the same phenomenon is ubiquitous at home: high-resolution UV-optical VLT/UVES spectra of about 20 solar-system comets collected since 2002 between 0.68 and 3.25 AU show up to about 40 Fe I and 25 Ni I emission lines, with Ni and Fe of the same order of magnitude (unlike the solar/meteoritic ratio, which runs ~10× more Fe). The paper weighs several release mechanisms — partial sublimation of Ni-rich sulfides such as pentlandite (needs >600 K), superheating of submicrometre fluffy aggregates, nanoparticle impact vapor (~1000 K) — and, on the strength of the Fe/Ni–carbon-oxide correlation, favors sublimation of low-temperature organometallic carriers, specifically the carbonyls Fe(CO)5 and Ni(CO)4, whose sublimation temperatures are only slightly above that of CO2 (later A&A work puts them near ~100 K, between CO2 and H2O); Ni(CO)4 sublimates ~10× faster than Fe(CO)5 near 300 K, which explains the cometary Ni overabundance. This pairing is the standard mainstream rebuttal invoked against the 3I/ATLAS 'nickel without iron' claim — and later VLT work (Hutsemékers et al. 2026, A&A 706, A43) showed 3I/ATLAS's log(Ni I/Fe I) fell from 1.27 ± 0.10 at 2.64 AU (the first epoch at which Fe I was detected at all; only lower limits, >0.99, exist at 3.78 and 3.14 AU) to ~0.3 near 1.9–2.0 AU, becoming indistinguishable from solar-system comets and from 2I/Borisov.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"spectrum","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.09305","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03485-4","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03435-0","https://www.eso.org/public/archives/releases/sciencepapers/eso2108/eso2108a.pdf"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-03","title":"March 2020 outburst and the transient 'double nucleus' — a textbook comet splitting event","date":"2020-03-30","observation":"Drahus et al. (ATel #13549, OGLE 1.3-m, Las Campanas) recorded two consecutive outbursts — ~0.3 mag between UT 2020 March 4.3 and 5.3 and ~0.4 mag between March 8.3 and 9.3, ~0.7 mag total in 5 days. Jewitt, Kim, Mutchler et al. (ApJL 896, L39), whose photometry independently shows a steady brightening from January 3 to February 24 indicating rising activity, tied the outburst to solids ejected with the comet outbound at ~2.8 AU: combined cross-section ~100 km^2 and ~2 × 10^7 kg in 0.1 mm grains — roughly 10^-4 of the nucleus mass for an assumed 500 m nucleus radius. HST then resolved a transient secondary on 2020-03-30 (cross-section ~0.6 km^2, dust mass ~10^5 kg). The secondary was absent in images on and before March 28 and gone again on and after April 3.","loeb_take":"Loeb has not treated the outburst or splitting as anomalous; both are standard comet behavior and are among the reasons he classes Borisov as a 'classical comet.' In his scale paper Borisov's coma onset and volatile-driven activity are cited as the evidence placing it in the green zone (Level 0), in direct contrast with 1I (no coma at all) and 3I (which he argues showed activity inconsistent with its spectrum).","loeb_quote":"In stark contrast, Borisov presented as a classical comet despite its interstellar origin","quote_source":"Eldadi, Tenenbaum & Loeb, 'The Loeb Scale: Astronomical Classification of Interstellar Objects', arXiv:2508.09167, 2025-08","official_explanation":"Jewitt et al. attribute the delayed appearance and rapid disappearance of the secondary to rotational bursting of one or more large (meter-sized) boulders under outgassing torques after ejection from the main nucleus — not a nucleus break-up. They characterize the events as 'minor events involving a negligible fraction of the total mass,' with the comet surviving the planetary region essentially intact. Outbursts and fragment shedding are common in solar-system comets.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"lightcurve","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.01242","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab99cb","https://www.astronomerstelegram.org/?read=13549","http://www2.ess.ucla.edu/~jewitt/papers/2020/JKM20.pdf"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-04","title":"'May be the first truly pristine comet ever observed' — anomalously high, spatially uniform polarization","date":"2021-03-30","observation":"Bagnulo et al. (Nature Communications 12, 1797) imaged Borisov in polarimetric mode with VLT/FORS2 from 2019-12-25 to 2020-03-20. Measured coma polarization Pr (R_SPECIAL filter) fell from 3.76 ± 0.08% at phase angle 28.48 deg (2019-12-25) through 1.19 ± 0.21% at 24.14 deg (2020-02-06) to -0.82 ± 0.13% at 18.06 deg (2020-03-20), with V- and I-filter values consistent (e.g. V: 3.35 ± 0.07% at 28.48 deg) — systematically higher than typical solar-system comets at matching phase angles. The only solar-system object with a comparable polarimetric curve is C/1995 O1 (Hale-Bopp), and unlike Hale-Bopp (whose polarimetric images showed jets and arcs), Borisov's coma is polarimetrically homogeneous.","loeb_take":"Loeb does not contest this result and has not framed Borisov's dust as anomalous in the technosignature sense. His consistent public position is that Borisov is the natural interstellar comet against which 1I and 3I should be judged — pristine but ordinary in kind. No Borisov-specific Loeb quote on the polarimetry exists; his general characterization is that Borisov 'appeared familiar', and his Loeb-scale paper (arXiv:2508.09167) rates Borisov Level 0 versus Level 4 for both 1I/'Oumuamua and 3I/ATLAS.","loeb_quote":"Among the four interstellar objects, only Borisov appeared familiar.","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'The Unfamiliar Nature of Interstellar Objects', Medium, Sep 2022 — https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-unfamiliar-nature-of-interstellar-objects-de8c855677e4","official_explanation":"ESO and the authors read the high, uniform polarization as evidence of an unprocessed, compositionally homogeneous dust population: Borisov apparently never passed close to any star before its 2019 solar passage, so its grains were never thermally or radiatively altered — making it more pristine than Hale-Bopp, which had made one prior solar passage (~2250 BCE). Co-author Alberto Cellino's polarization-plus-color analysis concluded the formation environment was not so different in composition from the early Solar System.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"polarization","verify":"CORRECTED","sources":["https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-22000-x","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8009915/","https://www.eso.org/public/news/eso2106/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.09167"]},{"object":"2i","id":"B-05","title":"Compact sub-kilometre nucleus — small, ordinary, and tightly bracketed","date":"2019-12-11","observation":"Jewitt, Hui, Kim et al. (arXiv:1912.05422, published ApJL 888, L23) modelled HST high-resolution imaging and found the scattering is dominated by a coma of large particles (characteristic size ~0.1 mm) ejected anisotropically. Convolution modelling of the coma surface-brightness profile sets a robust upper limit of spherical-equivalent nucleus radius rn <= 0.5 km (geometric albedo 0.04 assumed); an independent constraint from the measured non-gravitational acceleration gives rn > 0.2 km (density 500 kg m^-3 assumed). So 0.2 km < rn <= 0.5 km, i.e. a few hundred metres.","loeb_take":"Loeb accepts the sub-kilometre nucleus at face value and uses it purely as a scale reference: he describes Borisov's nucleus as 'about a few hundred meters in radius,' and in his 3I/ATLAS essays states that 3I's nucleus is roughly a thousand times more massive than Borisov's and about a million times more massive than 1I/'Oumuamua. For Borisov the size is a datum, not an anomaly.","loeb_quote":"about a few hundred meters in radius","quote_source":"Avi Loeb, 'The Unfamiliar Nature of Interstellar Objects,' Medium, 2022-09-23","official_explanation":"A few-hundred-metre icy nucleus with a large-grain, anisotropically ejected dust coma is entirely typical of active solar-system comets; the non-gravitational acceleration is fully accounted for by measured outgassing (unlike 1I/'Oumuamua, where acceleration occurred with no detected coma). Independent estimates (e.g. Hui et al. 2020; VISIR thermal work) converge on the same few-hundred-metre range.","loeb_scale":0,"viz_hint":"size","verify":"CONFIRMED","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.05422","https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ab621b","http://www2.ess.ucla.edu/~jewitt/papers/2020/JHK20.pdf","https://avi-loeb.medium.com/the-unfamiliar-nature-of-interstellar-objects-de8c855677e4"]}],"timeline":[{"date":"2025-05-07","title":"TESS pre-discovery detections begin","kind":"observation","description":"NASA's TESS spacecraft serendipitously imaged 3I/ATLAS from 2025-05-07 to 2025-06-02, 55 days before discovery, at roughly 6.4 AU from the Sun. Precovery photometry suggests the object was already active at ~6 AU, implying sublimation of ices more volatile than water.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-05-22","title":"Earliest ZTF precovery images","kind":"observation","description":"Zwicky Transient Facility precovery detections date back to 2025-05-22 (identified on individual unstacked images by Seligman et al.), with additional detection runs 2025-06-14 to 2025-06-21 and 2025-06-22 to 2025-06-29 identified within hours of the discovery report. ATLAS's own archives held precoveries from 2025-06-05 to 2025-06-29.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-06-21","title":"Vera C. Rubin Observatory serendipitous imaging","kind":"observation","description":"The Rubin Observatory captured 3I/ATLAS by chance during science-validation imaging starting 2025-06-21, ten days before discovery — among the highest-quality early data on the object.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-07-01","title":"Discovery by ATLAS, Rio Hurtado, Chile","kind":"discovery","description":"The ATLAS survey telescope at Rio Hurtado, Chile (station W68) discovered the object at apparent magnitude ~18, 4.51 AU from the Sun and 3.50 AU from Earth, inbound at ~61 km/s relative to the Sun.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-07-02","title":"Confirmed interstellar — designated 3I/ATLAS (C/2025 N1)","kind":"discovery","description":"Follow-up astrometry confirmed a strongly hyperbolic orbit (eccentricity 6.141, hyperbolic excess velocity ~58 km/s — faster than 1I/'Oumuamua at 26 km/s and 2I/Borisov at 32 km/s). The Minor Planet Center announced it as the third confirmed interstellar object; Nordic Optical Telescope and Teide Observatory confirmed cometary activity the same day.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-07-16","title":"Loeb raises 'alien technology' hypothesis; anomaly list begins","kind":"statement","description":"Avi Loeb (with Adam Hibberd and Adam Crowl) posted the arXiv paper 'Is the Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS Alien Technology?' (arXiv:2507.12213, v1 2025-07-16), starting a running numbered list of anomalies (retrograde ecliptic-aligned trajectory ~0.2% probability, fine-tuned Mars/Jupiter arrival geometry, etc.) that grew to 22 items by March 2026. NASA/ESA and most astronomers maintained it is a natural comet throughout.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-07-21","title":"Hubble images constrain nucleus size","kind":"observation","description":"Hubble Space Telescope imaging constrained the nucleus diameter to 0.32-5.6 km and revealed a sunward dust fan / anti-solar tail. Later non-gravitational-acceleration analyses pointed to a sub-kilometer nucleus — roughly 0.8-1.1 km diameter and mass ~1.6e11 kg if the acceleration is driven by CO2 outgassing (Thoss, Loeb & Burkert).","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-08-06","title":"JWST: unusually CO2-rich composition","kind":"observation","description":"JWST NIRSpec observations (results announced 2025-08-25) found a coma unusually rich in carbon dioxide — CO2/H2O mixing ratio 8.0±1.0, among the highest ever measured — with CO2 at 129±1 kg/s, water 6.6±0.2 kg/s, CO 14.0±0.9 kg/s, plus water ice and carbonyl sulfide.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-08-21","title":"SPHEREx maps giant CO2 coma; VLT confirms cyanide and nickel","kind":"observation","description":"NASA's SPHEREx (observing 2025-08-08 to 08-12) reported a CO2 gas coma spanning at least 348,000 km in radius (~70 kg/s CO2) plus water ice. Concurrent VLT spectroscopy confirmed cyanide (17.6±2.0 g/s) alongside atomic nickel (4.6±0.7 g/s, first detected 2025-07-20) with no accompanying iron vapor — the nickel-without-iron signature became Loeb anomaly material, though nickel-carbonyl chemistry in normal comets can explain it.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-10-03","title":"Mars close approach — 0.194 AU; imaged by Mars orbiters","kind":"close_approach","description":"3I/ATLAS passed 0.194 AU (29.0 million km) from Mars at 04:38 UT. NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter HiRISE imaged it at ~30 km/pixel (a diffuse, comet-consistent smear), ESA's ExoMars TGO CaSSIS captured the coma in 5-second exposures, and MAVEN detected hydrogen in the UV. HiRISE image release was delayed ~6 weeks by the US government shutdown.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-10-21","title":"Solar conjunction — unobservable from Earth","kind":"status","description":"The comet passed behind the Sun as seen from Earth around 2025-10-21, making its perihelion unobservable from the ground; Earth-based observers lost it for roughly late October through early November 2025 (solar elongation under ~25 deg from 2025-10-04 to 2025-11-06). Spacecraft (including GOES-19 and Mars/solar assets) provided partial coverage.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-10-29","title":"Perihelion — 1.36 AU from the Sun","kind":"close_approach","description":"Perihelion at 11:45 UT on 2025-10-29 at 1.356 AU (203 million km), between the orbits of Earth and Mars, moving at its maximum speed of ~68 km/s. It brightened faster near perihelion than typical comets — item 22 on Loeb's anomaly list, attributed by most astronomers to hypervolatile-driven outgassing.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-11-08","title":"Post-conjunction reemergence in the morning sky","kind":"observation","description":"Ground-based observers recovered the comet after solar conjunction; astrophotographer Michael Jaeger imaged the revived dust tail on 2025-11-08. Post-perihelion astrometry soon showed non-gravitational acceleration peaking at (89.3±4.6)e-9 AU/day^2 about a month before perihelion — normal for a small actively-outgassing nucleus, but logged by Loeb as an anomaly for its sideways component.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-11-19","title":"NASA press event: 'it's a comet' — HiRISE, MAVEN, Hubble, JWST data released","kind":"statement","description":"After the government shutdown ended, NASA held a live press event releasing spacecraft imagery (HiRISE at 30 km/pixel, MAVEN UV hydrogen detection, Hubble, JWST, SPHEREx) and stating 3I/ATLAS 'exhibits behaviors typical of comets' — gas and dust shedding, gravitational response — i.e., a natural comet. Loeb publicly criticized the conference for dismissing his anomalies. ESA's JUICE spacecraft also observed the comet during November 2025 en route to Jupiter.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-12-19","title":"Earth closest approach — 1.80 AU","kind":"close_approach","description":"Closest approach to Earth at 06:01 UT on 2025-12-19 at 1.798 AU (269 million km), out beyond the 4:1 Kirkwood gap of the asteroid belt, at apparent magnitude ~19.9 — never any threat to Earth. Around the same period a second JWST epoch detected methane that had been absent pre-perihelion.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2025-12-19","title":"Radio technosignature searches come up empty (ATA + MeerKAT)","kind":"observation","description":"A SETI Institute / Allen Telescope Array search (Sheikh et al., arXiv:2512.18142, submitted 2025-12-19) scanned 1-9 GHz for 7.25 hours around the Earth close approach: ~74 million narrowband hits reduced to 211 candidates by RFI filtering, all rejected — no technosignatures, with EIRP upper limits of ~10-110 W across the band. An earlier MeerKAT search (900-1670 MHz) had likewise found nothing, with a minimum EIRP limit of ~0.17 W (below cellphone-handset power).","object":"3i"},{"date":"2026-01-22","title":"Opposition — best final viewing geometry","kind":"observation","description":"3I/ATLAS reached opposition at 177.6 deg solar elongation, visible all night but fading; Loeb separately flagged the 2026-01-22 alignment with the Sun-Earth axis to within 0.69 deg (phase angle under 2 deg for about a week) as a rare chance to measure the opposition surge of its dust. Large telescopes continued photometric monitoring of the outbound comet.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2026-03-12","title":"Loeb publishes final tally of 22 anomalies","kind":"statement","description":"As the comet faded, Loeb's Medium essay enumerated 22 anomalies across mass/geometry, jet behavior, composition (nickel>iron, unusual isotopes), and trajectory coincidences (including a claimed 0.6%-probability alignment within 9 deg of the 1977 'Wow! Signal' arrival direction), ranking it Level 4 on his own 'Loeb Scale.' Mainstream rebuttals (e.g., Penn State's AstroWright) argued each item is explicable for a natural comet.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2026-03-16","title":"Jupiter close approach — 0.358 AU; Juno stays home","kind":"close_approach","description":"Closest approach to Jupiter at 12:22 UT at 0.358 AU (53.6 million km); it also passed 0.204 AU from the small Jovian moon Eupheme on 2026-03-17. A proposed Juno intercept (Loeb/Hibberd, arXiv:2507.21402 — requiring a ~2.7 km/s burn in September 2025) was never executed: Juno was low on fuel with engine problems and was not redirected; no Juno flyby observations resulted.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2026-04-02","title":"ESA JUICE results: an ordinary comet seen from close range","kind":"observation","description":"ESA released JUICE observations taken in November 2025, days after perihelion, from ~60 million km (JANUS camera and NavCam plus the MAJIS, SWI, and UVS spectrometers): a distinct coma with gas and dust extending over 5 million km, separate dust and anti-solar tails, and water output of ~2,000 kg/s on 2025-11-02 — equivalent to ~70 Olympic swimming pools per day, mostly from the sunward side — all consistent with a normal, if volatile-rich, comet.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2026-04-23","title":"Loeb status essay: a fading dot at 6.7 AU","kind":"statement","description":"Loeb's 'Where is 3I/ATLAS Now?' put the comet at ~1 billion km (6.7 AU) from the Sun, well beyond Jupiter, 'heading away from us back to interstellar space' and nearing the detectability horizon; he reorganized his claims into 12 anomaly categories at 'Loeb Scale' Level 4, while conceding the object is leaving without any close inspection.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2026-05-15","title":"Second solar conjunction — ground observability ends","kind":"status","description":"Around May 2026 (date approximate) 3I/ATLAS entered its second solar conjunction, ending routine ground-based observation. When it reemerges around September 2026 it will be beyond Saturn's orbit and fainter than magnitude 20 — effectively the end of its observable apparition for all but the largest telescopes.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2026-06-15","title":"Published ATA study reaffirms: no signals","kind":"statement","description":"The peer-reviewed Allen Telescope Array result (published in The Astronomical Journal, revised May 2026) circulated widely in June 2026 (date approximate): a seven-hour, 1-9 GHz scan found no technosignatures from 3I/ATLAS, closing out the SETI angle as the object receded. Loeb continued essays into June 2026 (e.g., on dust 'windshield' effects) but no new observational anomalies emerged.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2026-07-17","title":"Current status: nearing Saturn's orbital distance, outbound for good","kind":"status","description":"As of today 3I/ATLAS is about 9.25 AU from the Sun (~1.38 billion km; 10.25 AU from Earth per JPL Horizons), reaching Saturn's ~9.5 AU orbital distance in late July while hidden in solar conjunction (solar elongation ~9 deg) — unobservable and fainter than magnitude 20. It is escaping on its hyperbolic trajectory at ~58 km/s excess velocity, nearly symmetric to its inbound path; it will pass the inner Oort cloud region (~2,000 AU) around the year 2189 and leave the Sun's influence entirely in a few thousand years. Consensus verdict: the most-studied interstellar object ever, and a natural — though unusually CO2-rich, nickel-emitting, and ancient (possibly 7-14 billion years old) — comet.","object":"3i"},{"date":"2017-09-09","title":"Perihelion at 0.256 AU (pre-discovery)","kind":"close_approach","description":"'Oumuamua made its closest approach to the Sun at 0.256 AU (inside Mercury's orbit), moving at ~87.7 km/s at perihelion — far too fast for the Sun to retain it. Nothing on Earth saw it: perihelion occurred ~40 days before discovery, so the entire inbound leg and the solar flyby were unobserved. This gap later mattered enormously — every acceleration measurement and every thermal model had to extrapolate back through a perihelion no one witnessed.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2017-10-14","title":"Closest approach to Earth at ~0.162 AU (still undetected)","kind":"close_approach","description":"The object passed ~0.16175 AU (~24.2 million km, about 63 lunar distances) from Earth — its minimum Earth distance — five days before anyone found it. It was detected only on the outbound leg, already receding, which compressed the entire global observing campaign into a few frantic weeks before it faded beyond reach of ground-based telescopes.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2017-10-19","title":"Discovery by Robert Weryk / Pan-STARRS1","kind":"discovery","description":"Robert Weryk found the fast-moving ~20th-magnitude object (it had peaked at magnitude 19.7 the night before) in Pan-STARRS1 survey images from Haleakala, Hawaii, while searching for near-Earth asteroids. Follow-up astrometry (including pre-discovery images from Oct 18) quickly showed an eccentricity of ~1.2 — the first solidly hyperbolic small body ever recorded, meaning it came from outside the Solar System. Initially designated comet C/2017 U1 (PANSTARRS).","object":"1i"},{"date":"2017-11-06","title":"Designation saga ends: first-ever 'I' class — 1I/'Oumuamua","kind":"status","description":"A two-week identity crisis: designated comet C/2017 U1 at discovery, reclassified asteroid A/2017 U1 on Oct 25 when deep imaging showed no coma or tail, then on Nov 6 the Minor Planet Center (MPEC 2017-V17) created an entirely new designation class — 'I' for interstellar — making it 1I/'Oumuamua, with the IAU-approved Hawaiian name meaning roughly 'a messenger from afar arriving first' (suggested by the Pan-STARRS team). No object had ever forced the IAU to invent a new category before.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2017-11-20","title":"VLT/Gemini lightcurve: extreme 10:1 elongation (Meech et al., Nature)","kind":"observation","description":"Meech et al. published the coordinated ESO VLT / Gemini / CFHT / UKIRT / Keck lightcurve campaign in Nature: brightness varied by a factor of ~10 every 7.3 hours, implying an axis ratio of at least ~10:1 (later refined to ~6:1–8:1) — more elongated than any known Solar System asteroid or comet. Reddish surface, no dust, no coma, tumbling rotation. The 'cigar' (or 'pancake', per later modeling) shape became anomaly #1 in the alien-tech debate.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2017-12-13","title":"Breakthrough Listen scans 'Oumuamua with Green Bank — radio silence","kind":"observation","description":"Breakthrough Listen pointed the 100-m Green Bank Telescope at 'Oumuamua for the first of four observing blocks (3:45–9:45 pm ET), sweeping 1–12 GHz across the L, S, C and X bands and accumulating 90 TB of raw data over the ~2-hour on-target observation. Sensitivity was good enough to detect a transmitter with the power of a cellphone at the object's distance in under a minute. No artificial signals were found then or in the completed analysis (Enriquez et al. 2018) — the natural-vs-artificial question would instead be fought over dynamics, not radio.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2018-06-27","title":"Non-gravitational acceleration detected (Micheli et al., Nature)","kind":"observation","description":"Micheli et al. reported in Nature that 'Oumuamua's trajectory — reconstructed from ground data plus late Hubble astrometry through January 2018 — required a non-gravitational acceleration of ~4.9e-6 m/s^2 at 1 AU, falling off roughly as 1/r^2. For a comet this is normal outgassing; for an object with no detected coma, dust, or tail it was a paradox. This single measurement is the load-bearing anomaly behind every exotic hypothesis that followed, from hydrogen icebergs to lightsails.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2018-10-26","title":"Bialy & Loeb lightsail paper ignites global media storm","kind":"statement","description":"Shmuel Bialy and Avi Loeb (Harvard CfA) posted 'Could Solar Radiation Pressure Explain 'Oumuamua's Peculiar Acceleration?' (arXiv:1810.11490, published ApJL 868 L1 on 2018-11-12). They showed radiation pressure could produce the observed acceleration if the object were a thin sheet with mass-to-area ratio ~0.1 g/cm^2 — about 0.3–0.9 mm thick, a lightsail geometry — and floated that it 'may be a fully operational probe sent intentionally to Earth vicinity by an alien civilization.' The paper triggered a worldwide media storm and made Loeb the public face of the alien-artifact hypothesis.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2018-11-14","title":"Spitzer non-detection caps the size and rules out normal outgassing","kind":"observation","description":"Trilling et al. (AJ; NASA release Nov 14, 2018) reported that Spitzer stared at 'Oumuamua's position for 30.2 hours on 2017 Nov 21–22 and saw nothing in the infrared. The non-detection capped the size (effective radius roughly 49–220 m depending on albedo, implying a possibly highly reflective surface) and set tight limits on CO/CO2 outgassing — deepening the acceleration paradox: whatever pushed it was not detectable as gas or dust. Bialy & Loeb leaned on these limits.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2019-07-01","title":"ISSI team verdict: 'no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation' (Nature Astronomy)","kind":"statement","description":"The 14-member International Space Science Institute (ISSI) 'Oumuamua team ('The natural history of 'Oumuamua', Bannister et al., Nature Astronomy) published the mainstream consensus review: every observed property — shape, tumbling, color, acceleration — lies within plausible ranges for a natural interstellar planetesimal, and 'we find no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation.' Loeb publicly rejected the conclusion; the paper remains the standard natural-origin reference against which his claims are measured.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2020-06-09","title":"Seligman & Laughlin hydrogen-iceberg hypothesis (ApJL)","kind":"statement","description":"Darryl Seligman and Gregory Laughlin proposed (ApJL 896 L8; arXiv:2005.12932) that 'Oumuamua was a hydrogen iceberg formed in the coldest cores of giant molecular clouds: H2 outgassing is nearly invisible (no dust, no detectable coma) yet provides the acceleration, and H2 sublimation sculpting naturally explains the extreme elongation. Critics (including Loeb, and Hoang & Loeb 2020) countered that H2 ice could not survive the interstellar journey — but the paper opened the 'exotic ice' family of natural explanations.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2021-01-26","title":"Loeb publishes 'Extraterrestrial' — the case goes mainstream","kind":"statement","description":"Avi Loeb's book 'Extraterrestrial: The First Sign of Intelligent Life Beyond Earth' (Houghton Mifflin Harcourt) hit shelves and became an international bestseller, arguing 'Oumuamua's anomalies — the acceleration without a coma, the extreme shape, the unusual initial rest-frame trajectory — are best explained as artificial technology, possibly a defunct lightsail. The book transformed a technical dispute into a public referendum on scientific open-mindedness and directly seeded Loeb's Galileo Project (announced July 2021).","object":"1i"},{"date":"2021-03-16","title":"Desch & Jackson: nitrogen-ice fragment of an exo-Pluto (JGR Planets)","kind":"statement","description":"Steven Desch and Alan Jackson (ASU) published paired papers in JGR: Planets (AGU release Mar 16, 2021) proposing 'Oumuamua was a chip of solid nitrogen ice blasted off a Pluto-like exoplanet ~half a billion years ago: N2 sublimation matches the acceleration with no visible coma, and the high albedo of N2 ice allows a smaller body. Loeb and collaborators (Siraj & Loeb) rebutted that the required mass of exo-Pluto ejecta strains the galactic budget; Desch defended the numbers. The 'nitrogen iceberg' remains a leading natural candidate.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2023-03-22","title":"Bergner & Seligman 'dark comet' H2 answer in Nature — and Loeb's rebuttal","kind":"statement","description":"Jennifer Bergner (Berkeley) and Darryl Seligman published in Nature that cosmic-ray radiolysis of ordinary water ice during 'Oumuamua's interstellar transit created trapped molecular hydrogen; solar warming then released the H2, producing the acceleration with no visible coma — no exotic composition needed. Widely reported as 'mystery solved.' Within days Thiem Hoang and Avi Loeb posted a rebuttal (arXiv:2303.13861; Loeb's Medium essay ''Oumuamua Was Not a Hydrogen-Water Iceberg', Mar 2023) arguing that evaporative cooling by the escaping H2 leaves the surface a factor of ~9 colder than Bergner & Seligman assumed, cutting the H2 thrust below what is required; Bergner countered that the cooling effects do not change the conclusion. The dispute remains unresolved in print.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2025-08-06","title":"Loeb Scale retroactively ranks 'Oumuamua Level 4 (vs Borisov Level 0)","kind":"statement","description":"Amid the 3I/ATLAS debate, Loeb formalized 'The Loeb Scale: Astronomical Classification of Interstellar Objects' (arXiv:2508.09167, submitted Aug 6, 2025; later published in the International Journal of Astrobiology) — a 0-10 anomaly scale for interstellar objects modeled on the Torino Scale (Levels 0-3 natural, 4-7 increasingly anomalous, 8-10 confirmed artificial). Applied retroactively: 1I/'Oumuamua scores Level 4 (multiple unexplained anomalies), 2I/Borisov Level 0 (the perfectly natural control comet), and 3I/ATLAS Level 4. The scale is Loeb's institutionalization of his 1I-vs-2I framing: Borisov is what a normal interstellar comet looks like; 'Oumuamua is not that.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2026-07-24","title":"Outbound: 53.6 AU from the Sun, headed for Pegasus","kind":"status","description":"Per JPL Horizons, 'Oumuamua is 53.58 AU from the Sun as of today (2026-07-24) — beyond Pluto's average orbit, receding at ~27.0 km/s and asymptotically slowing toward its hyperbolic excess speed of 26.33 km/s, headed for the constellation Pegasus. It passed Neptune's orbital distance in 2022 and is far beyond recovery by any existing telescope; its true nature is now permanently unmeasurable unless a chase mission (e.g. a Project Lyra concept) is ever launched. Every remaining argument is fought over the 2017-2018 data alone.","object":"1i"},{"date":"2019-08-30","title":"Discovery by Gennadiy Borisov at MARGO, Crimea","kind":"discovery","description":"Amateur astronomer Gennadiy Borisov discovers a ~magnitude 17.8 comet at low solar elongation using his self-built 0.65 m telescope at his MARGO observatory (MPC code L51) in Nauchnij, Crimea — the first interstellar object ever found by an amateur, and found while it was still inbound (~3 AU from the Sun), unlike 'Oumuamua which was found outbound. It showed a condensed coma and short tail from the start: an unambiguously active comet.","object":"2i"},{"date":"2019-09-11","title":"Provisional designation C/2019 Q4 (Borisov); hyperbolic orbit suspected","kind":"status","description":"The Minor Planet Center announces the object as comet C/2019 Q4 (Borisov) (MPEC 2019-R106), noting the orbit solution 'has converged to the hyperbolic elements shown below, which would indicate an interstellar origin.' The first published solution gave eccentricity e = 3.08 — far beyond any bound solar-system orbit (later refined to e = 3.36) — with JPL's Farnocchia, Bill Gray, and Dave Tholen independently reaching the same conclusion: a likely interstellar comet flagged within two weeks of discovery.","object":"2i"},{"date":"2019-09-20","title":"First gas detected: cyanogen (CN) with the William Herschel Telescope","kind":"observation","description":"Fitzsimmons et al. detect the CN (0-0) emission band at 388 nm using the 4.2 m William Herschel Telescope + ISIS spectrograph on La Palma (2019 Sep 20.2 UT) — the first spectroscopic detection of gas from any interstellar object. CN production rate (3.7 +/- 0.4) x 10^24 molecules/s at r = 2.7 AU; follow-up work showed 2I is depleted in C2, but overall its gas chemistry looked like a normal solar-system comet (ApJL 885, L9; arXiv:1909.12144).","object":"2i"},{"date":"2019-09-24","title":"Confirmed interstellar: named 2I/Borisov (MPEC 2019-S72)","kind":"status","description":"The IAU/Minor Planet Center issues MPEC 2019-S72, confirming the interstellar hyperbolic orbit (e = 3.372 in the circular's solution, from 472 observations spanning Aug 30 - Sep 24) and assigning the permanent designation 2I/Borisov — the second interstellar object and the first unambiguous interstellar comet. The IAU retained the discoverer's name, honoring the amateur discovery.","object":"2i"},{"date":"2019-10-12","title":"First Hubble imaging: tiny nucleus, radius under 0.5 km","kind":"observation","description":"HST/WFC3 obtains the sharpest images yet (Jewitt, Luu et al.), beginning a monitoring campaign through perihelion. Convolution modeling of the coma profile sets a nucleus radius limit of r <= 0.5 km (with r > 0.2 km from the non-gravitational acceleration constraint) — roughly 15x smaller than early ground-based estimates (0.7-3.3 km), per NASA's release. A small, ordinary comet nucleus wrapped in dust (Jewitt et al. 2020, ApJL 888, L23; arXiv:1912.05422).","object":"2i"},{"date":"2019-12-08","title":"Perihelion at 2.006 AU","kind":"close_approach","description":"2I/Borisov reaches perihelion at 2.006 AU (just outside Mars' orbit), moving ~44 km/s — well above the ~30 km/s local solar escape speed, with a hyperbolic excess velocity of ~32 km/s that guarantees it will leave the solar system. Throughout, it behaves as a classic active comet — sustained water and CO outgassing, dust coma and tail — the natural 'control case' against 'Oumuamua's inert, accelerating anomaly. Hubble imaged it near perihelion on Dec 9.","object":"2i"},{"date":"2019-12-28","title":"Closest approach to Earth: ~1.9 AU","kind":"close_approach","description":"The comet makes its closest approach to Earth at roughly 1.9 AU (~290 million km) — never a threat and never close, but well-placed for the VLT, ALMA, and Hubble campaigns that bracketed this date: ALMA had observed Dec 15-16, and Bagnulo's VLT/FORS2 polarimetric monitoring spanned late December 2019 through March 2020.","object":"2i"},{"date":"2020-03-09","title":"Double outburst: two brightenings in five days","kind":"observation","description":"Between March 4-9, 2020 (outbound, ~2.8 AU), Polish astronomers Drahus and Guzik (Jagiellonian University) with the OGLE team report the comet brightening ~0.7 magnitudes in two distinct outbursts within days — ~0.3 mag between Mar 4.3-5.3 and ~0.4 mag between Mar 8.3-9.3 UT (ATel #13549, issued Mar 12). Jewitt et al. later measured the ejected dust: ~100 km^2 cross-section, ~2 x 10^7 kg — a warning sign of nucleus instability after perihelion heating.","object":"2i"},{"date":"2020-03-30","title":"Hubble catches the nucleus splitting: transient double nucleus","kind":"observation","description":"HST/WFC3 images from two programs (PI Jewitt, ATel #13611; PI Bolin, ATel #13613, announced early April 2020) reveal a fragment ~0.3 arcsec from the optocenter. A transient 'double nucleus' with ~0.6 km^2 cross-section (~10^5 kg of dust) appears on March 30 — absent March 28 and gone by April 3. Jewitt, Kim & Mutchler interpret it as rotational bursting of meter-sized boulders ejected in the March outburst, not catastrophic disruption: the comet shed a splinter but survived (ApJL 896, L39).","object":"2i"},{"date":"2020-04-20","title":"ALMA: unusually CO-rich coma (Cordiner et al., Nature Astronomy)","kind":"observation","description":"Cordiner, Milam et al. publish ALMA observations (taken 2019 Dec 15-16) detecting HCN and CO — the first direct measurement of gas from another planetary system. CO is 9-26x more abundant relative to water than in average solar-system comets, suggesting 2I/Borisov formed in an extremely cold, CO-ice-rich outer region of its home system, perhaps around a carbon-rich or M-dwarf star (Nature Astronomy 4, 861; the chemically distinct fingerprint of an alien comet).","object":"2i"},{"date":"2021-03-30","title":"VLT polarimetry: 'the most pristine comet ever observed' (Bagnulo et al.)","kind":"observation","description":"Bagnulo et al. publish VLT/FORS2 polarimetry (taken Dec 2019 - Mar 2020) in Nature Communications: 2I/Borisov's polarization exceeds that of all typical comets and its coma is polarimetrically homogeneous, resembling only the unique comet Hale-Bopp. Conclusion: it likely never passed close to any star before ours — possibly the first truly pristine comet ever observed, carrying unaltered material from its birth cloud (Nat. Commun. 12, 1797).","object":"2i"},{"date":"2021-05-19","title":"Atomic nickel vapor in a cold coma (Guzik & Drahus, Nature)","kind":"observation","description":"Guzik & Drahus report gaseous atomic nickel in 2I/Borisov's coma at 2.322 AU (~180 K equilibrium temperature), produced at 0.9 +/- 0.3 x 10^22 atoms/s, likely from photolysis of a short-lived nickel-bearing molecule (Nature 593, 375). Heavy-metal vapor had previously been seen only in ultrahot environments like sungrazing comets. Published back-to-back with Manfroid et al.'s discovery of Fe and Ni vapor in ~20 solar-system comets — a shared, previously unnoticed chemistry linking 2I to home-grown comets and reinforcing its natural-comet status.","object":"2i"},{"date":"2025-08-06","title":"Loeb Scale ranks 2I/Borisov Level 0: the natural control case","kind":"statement","description":"In the wake of 3I/ATLAS, the Interstellar Object Significance Scale ('Loeb Scale') paper by Omer Eldadi, Gershon Tenenbaum, and Avi Loeb (arXiv:2508.09167, submitted Aug 6, 2025; later Int. J. Astrobiology) retroactively scores the three known interstellar objects on its 0-10 scale: 1I/'Oumuamua Level 4 (anomalous), 3I/ATLAS Level 4, and 2I/Borisov Level 0 — fully consistent with natural cometary behavior. Loeb has consistently treated Borisov as the benchmark 'ordinary comet' proving the detection pipeline works, sharpening the contrast with 'Oumuamua's anomalies.","object":"2i"},{"date":"2026-07-24","title":"Outbound: receding toward interstellar space","kind":"status","description":"2I/Borisov is far beyond observability, now tens of AU from the Sun and departing on its hyperbolic trajectory at ~32 km/s excess velocity, never to return. Its legacy: it confirmed that other planetary systems make comets much like ours (CN, HCN, dust, outbursts, nickel vapor), yet with distinct fingerprints (extreme CO enrichment, C2 depletion, unusually pristine dust) — the well-behaved sibling that makes 'Oumuamua's and 3I/ATLAS's oddities stand out.","object":"2i"}],"compare":[{"designation":"1I/2017 U1","name":"'Oumuamua","discovered":"2017-10-19, Pan-STARRS1 (Haleakala, Hawaii) — found ~40 days after perihelion, on the way out","eccentricity":1.20113,"inclination_deg":122.74,"perihelion_au":0.2559,"perihelion_date":"2017-09-09","closest_earth_au":0.1618,"v_infinity_kms":26.33,"size_estimate":"~100-400 m long (est. 100-1,000 m range), width/thickness ~35-167 m; extreme axis ratio ~6:1 to 10:1 (cigar or pancake shape)","nature":"Inert/rocky-appearing; no detected coma, dust, or gas despite deep searches — yet accelerated like a comet. Composition still debated (nitrogen ice, hydrogen ice, dark organic-rich crust all proposed)","weird_notes":["Non-gravitational acceleration (~5x10^-6 m/s^2 at 1 AU, radial ~2.8x10^-7 AU/d^2) with NO visible outgassing — no coma, no dust, no detected gas to explain it","Extreme lightcurve amplitude ~2.5 magnitudes implying axis ratio of at least 6:1, more elongated than any known solar-system asteroid or comet","Tumbling (non-principal-axis) rotation, period ~7.3-8 h","Red, comet-like surface color but zero cometary activity detected by Spitzer and deep ground-based imaging","Excess velocity vector close to the Local Standard of Rest — dynamically 'young' / unusual for an ejected planetesimal","Only detected post-perihelion at close range; observed arc was short (~2.5 months), so many properties remain unresolved"],"loeb_position":"Loeb (Bialy & Loeb 2018, ApJL) argued the non-gravitational acceleration without outgassing could be explained by solar radiation pressure on a thin, flat artificial object — a lightsail or discarded 'technological debris' from an alien civilization. He expanded this in his 2021 book 'Extraterrestrial,' maintaining the artificial-origin hypothesis is more plausible than exotic natural explanations like hydrogen icebergs."},{"designation":"2I/2019 Q4 (C/2019 Q4)","name":"Borisov","discovered":"2019-08-30, Gennadiy Borisov (amateur astronomer), 0.65-m MARGO observatory, Nauchnij, Crimea","eccentricity":3.356,"inclination_deg":44.05,"perihelion_au":2.007,"perihelion_date":"2019-12-08","closest_earth_au":1.9,"v_infinity_kms":32.2,"size_estimate":"Nucleus effective radius 0.2-0.5 km (HST); early ~975 m estimates were revised down. Coma/tail much larger (tail ~160,000 km)","nature":"Clearly cometary from first detection — bright coma and tail, normal dust and gas production. The 'ordinary' interstellar object; behaved like a moderately active solar-system comet","weird_notes":["Extremely CO-rich: CO/H2O ratio 3x+ higher than any comet measured in the inner solar system, suggesting formation beyond the CO ice line of a cold M-dwarf or outer disk (Bodewits et al. / Cordiner et al. 2020)","NH2-rich chemistry relative to typical comets","Unusually pristine dust — suggested it had never before passed close to any star","Nucleus fragmented/split in March 2020 after an outburst, post-perihelion (a normal thing for comets to do, but notable)","Otherwise the control case: proved interstellar comets can look exactly like ordinary comets"],"loeb_position":"Loeb accepted Borisov as a genuine natural comet and repeatedly uses it as his control case: 'Borisov looked like a comet from day one, which shows astronomers can recognize a comet when they see one — making 'Oumuamua's weirdness stand out.' He noted its pristine condition was mildly interesting but did not claim artificiality."},{"designation":"3I/ATLAS (C/2025 N1)","name":"ATLAS","discovered":"2025-07-01, ATLAS survey telescope, Rio Hurtado, Chile (prediscovery images: ATLAS back to 2025-06-05, ZTF to 2025-06-14; TESS imagery from May 2025 recovered later)","eccentricity":6.14135,"inclination_deg":175.12,"perihelion_au":1.35645,"perihelion_date":"2025-10-29 (11:45 UT); perihelion occurred behind the Sun as seen from Earth","closest_earth_au":1.798,"v_infinity_kms":58,"size_estimate":"Nucleus diameter 0.32-5.6 km (HST upper/lower bounds); non-gravitational-acceleration-based estimate ~0.82-1.05 km diameter (Forbes & Butler 2025, for density 0.5 g/cm^3); Loeb et al.'s pre-perihelion NGA upper limit instead implied a MINIMUM mass ~33 billion metric tons (3.3x10^13 kg) and diameter >~5 km — the two are in tension; rotation period 16.16 h","nature":"Active comet, CO2-dominated: JWST/NIRSpec (2025-08-06, 3.32 AU) measured CO2/H2O mixing ratio 7.6+/-0.3 (exceptionally high, 4.5-sigma above the comet trend), Q(CO2) 9.5x10^26 molecules/s (~70 kg/s) vs Q(H2O) 1.07x10^26 (~3 kg/s) pre-perihelion; also CO, carbonyl sulfide, water ice, later methane, plus nickel vapor. Likely an old, thick-crusted comet from the Milky Way thick disk. NASA/ESA and mainstream astronomy: natural comet","weird_notes":["Most eccentric orbit ever recorded for a solar-system-passing object: e = 6.14135 +/- 0.00002 (vs 1.2 for 1I, 3.36 for 2I) — v_inf ~58 km/s, faster than both predecessors","Retrograde orbit inclined only ~5 deg from the ecliptic plane (i = 175.12 deg) — Loeb puts chance alignment at ~0.2%","Trajectory threaded close to Mars (0.19 AU, 2025-10-03), Venus, and Jupiter (0.357 AU, 2026-03-16) while unobservable from Earth at perihelion","CO2/H2O ratio ~8 (7.6+/-0.3, JWST) — among the highest ever measured in any comet","Nickel-rich gas plume with anomalously low iron and Ni/CN ratio orders of magnitude above known comets (detected Aug 2025, VLT/UVES; Ni production ~0.9 g/s rising to ~5 g/s by 2.8 AU)","Pre-perihelion sunward anti-tail/collimated jet, later roughly symmetric jets post-perihelion; reported mini-jets separated ~120 deg","Non-gravitational acceleration detected at perihelion with a substantial non-radial (sideways) component (per Loeb's analysis of MPC/JPL astrometry)","Extreme negative polarization reported as unprecedented among known comets; near perihelion it brightened faster than known comets and appeared bluer than the Sun","Post-perihelion chemistry shift: methane emerged as ices faded — first CH4 detection on an interstellar object (Belyakov et al. 2026, ApJL, JWST/MIRI, Dec 2025 obs): CH4/H2O mixing ratio 11.0+/-0.5% rising to 21.6+/-1.3%, vs 0.1-10% for typical solar-system comets; separate June 2026 Nature paper (Cordiner et al., JWST isotopes: 12C/13C = 147, 14N/15N = 343) points to a cold, distant birth environment","Big and fast: nucleus estimated 3-5 orders of magnitude more massive than both 1I and 2I (Loeb: 'at least 100,000x Oumuamua'), in tension with expected interstellar-object mass distribution","Jupiter flyby 2026-03-16 at 0.357 AU passed as predicted on the gravitational trajectory (perijove 53.445 Mkm ~ Jupiter's Hill radius 53.502 Mkm — Loeb anomaly); ESA's Juice spacecraft (MAJIS, obs 2025-11-02, published 2026-04-02) measured water output ~2,000 kg/s (~70 Olympic pools/day) and a tail ~6 million km long","Status July 2026: outbound beyond Jupiter's distance in Gemini, magnitude ~15 and fading, leaving the solar system permanently (beyond Saturn's orbit and fainter than mag 20 by ~Sept 2026)"],"loeb_position":"Loeb maintained a running numbered anomaly file (reaching 22 anomalies by his March 2026 Medium summary '3I/ATLAS is Fading Away...'), assigning individual probabilities to coincidences (perijove ~ Jupiter's Hill radius, Wow! Signal alignment within 9 deg at 0.6% probability, ecliptic alignment, jet geometry). He rated it 4 on his 0-10 'Loeb scale' (10 = alien threat) — anomalous enough to warrant the technological hypothesis but 'slightly less anomalous' than 'Oumuamua — and repeatedly urged agencies to collect more data rather than dismiss the possibility; NASA/ESA and nearly all astronomers concluded it is a natural, unusually CO2-rich interstellar comet."}],"quotes":[{"text":"As of now, I assign a 30 to 40 percent likelihood that 3I/ATLAS does not have a fully natural origin","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-10-09","context":"Probability estimate of non-natural origin, as reported while anomaly count grew","source":"Futurism (futurism.com/space/astronomer-probability-alien-craft-comet)","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"This low-probability scenario includes the possibility of a black swan event akin to a Trojan Horse, where a technological object masquerades as a natural comet.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-10-09","context":"Explaining his 'black swan' framing of the technological hypothesis","source":"Futurism, quoting Loeb's Medium blog","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"As far as I know, there are no protocols for responding to the discovery of functioning alien devices near Earth.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-10-09","context":"On planetary preparedness if 3I/ATLAS proved technological","source":"Futurism, quoting Loeb","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"As largely a pedagogical exercise, in this paper we present additional analysis into the astrodynamics of 3I/ATLAS, and hypothesize that this object could be technological","speaker":"Avi Loeb (with Adam Hibberd, Adam Crowl)","date":"2025-07-16","context":"Abstract of the paper 'Is the Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS Alien Technology?'","source":"arXiv:2507.12213","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"possibly hostile as would be expected from the 'Dark Forest' resolution to the 'Fermi Paradox'","speaker":"Avi Loeb (with Adam Hibberd, Adam Crowl)","date":"2025-07-16","context":"The 'dark forest' line in the same arXiv paper's abstract","source":"arXiv:2507.12213","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"If it is technological — it may maneuver to benefit from the Sun's gravitational assist or release probes that could intercept the Earth or other planets owing to its retrograde orbit.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-10-03","context":"Warning about the perihelion pass hidden behind the Sun, in his Mars-flyby anomaly recap","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (A Recap of the Anomalies of 3I/ATLAS on the Day of Its Closest Approach to Mars)","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"If we find technological satellites of Jupiter that we did not send, it would imply that Jupiter is of interest to an extraterrestrial civilization.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2026-03","context":"On the March 16, 2026 Jupiter approach at ~Jupiter's Hill radius and the idea of released 'devices'","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (Jupiter-approach essays); IBTimes UK coverage","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"NASA's representatives should have emphasized what we do not understand about 3I/ATLAS rather than insist that it is a familiar comet from a new birth environment.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-11-19","context":"Reaction to NASA's post-shutdown press conference","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (There is Nothing More Deceptive than an Obvious Fact)","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"We should be humble about the little we know and curious about 3I/ATLAS rather than insist that we know its nature before data is shared and analyzed.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-11-03","context":"Essay inviting Kim Kardashian to join his research team, criticizing NASA data withholding","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (Kim Kardashian is Welcome to Join my Research Team on 3I/ATLAS)","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"Scientific truth is not decided by authority or popularity but by attention to data.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-11","context":"Response to astronomers insisting 3I/ATLAS is a natural comet","source":"Newsweek interview","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"It is the responsibility of scientists and NASA officials to acknowledge the existence of unexplained puzzles rather than display the arrogance of expertise while ignoring them.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2026-03-12","context":"Farewell essay as the object faded, tallying 22 anomalies and lowering it to Loeb Scale 3","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (3I/ATLAS is Fading Away, Leaving Us to Ponder Over Its 22 Mysterious Anomalies)","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"Given how well aligned its trajectory was with the orbital plane of Earth around the Sun, we might never witness a visitor like it in our lifetime.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2026-04-23","context":"Status essay with 3I/ATLAS a billion km out, well beyond Jupiter, leaving the solar system","source":"Avi Loeb, Medium (Where is 3I/ATLAS Now?)","camp":"loeb"},{"text":"This object is a comet. It looks and behaves like a comet, and all evidence points to it being a comet.","speaker":"Amit Kshatriya, NASA Associate Administrator","date":"2025-11-19","context":"NASA press conference releasing post-shutdown 3I/ATLAS imagery","source":"NASA live event; LiveNOW from FOX / Space.com coverage","camp":"official"},{"text":"We very much want to find signs of life in our universe... but 3I/ATLAS is a comet.","speaker":"Amit Kshatriya, NASA Associate Administrator","date":"2025-11-19","context":"Same NASA press conference, addressing alien-technology speculation directly","source":"NASA press conference; LiveNOW from FOX coverage","camp":"official"},{"text":"Everybody has got a camera and they're trying to get a picture of the ball. Nobody has the perfect view, and everybody has a different camera.","speaker":"Tom Statler, NASA lead scientist for solar system small bodies","date":"2025-11-19","context":"Baseball-stadium analogy for the multi-spacecraft observation campaign at the NASA press conference","source":"NASA press conference; Space.com coverage","camp":"official"},{"text":"Great question! NASA's observations show that this is the third interstellar comet to pass through our solar system. No aliens. No threat to life here on Earth.","speaker":"Sean Duffy, NASA Acting Administrator","date":"2025-11-03","context":"Reply on X to Kim Kardashian's question about 3I/ATLAS","source":"X (Twitter); quoted in Loeb's Medium essay and IBTimes UK","camp":"official"},{"text":"[paraphrase] Wait... what's the tea on 3I/ATLAS?!","speaker":"Kim Kardashian","date":"2025-11-03","context":"X post asking NASA about the interstellar object; outlets render the punctuation differently","source":"X (Twitter); IBTimes UK / Express Tribune coverage","camp":"media"},{"text":"Any suggestion that it's artificial is nonsense on stilts, and is an insult to the exciting work going on to understand this object.","speaker":"Chris Lintott, University of Oxford astronomer","date":"2025-07","context":"Reacting to Loeb's alien-technology paper shortly after discovery","source":"Live Science (Here we go again! Controversial paper...)","camp":"media"},{"text":"There have been numerous telescopic observations of 3I/ATLAS demonstrating that it's displaying classical signatures of cometary activity.","speaker":"Darryl Seligman, Michigan State University astronomer","date":"2025-07","context":"Lead author of the first characterization study, pushing back on the artificial hypothesis","source":"Live Science / Newsweek interviews","camp":"media"},{"text":"You have a piece of another star system that's close enough to home that we can actually study it in detail.","speaker":"Karen Meech, University of Hawaii astronomer","date":"2025-07","context":"On the scientific value of the third interstellar visitor after discovery","source":"University of Michigan News / phys.org","camp":"media"},{"text":"Considering an artificial origin, one possibility is that 'Oumuamua is a lightsail, floating in interstellar space as debris from an advanced technological equipment.","speaker":"Shmuel Bialy & Abraham Loeb","date":"2018-11-12","source":"Bialy & Loeb, 'Could Solar Radiation Pressure Explain 'Oumuamua's Peculiar Acceleration?', ApJL 868 L1 (arXiv:1810.11490, posted 2018-10-26)","camp":"loeb","context":"The lightsail sentence from the paper that launched the alien-technology hypothesis; verified verbatim against the arXiv full text."},{"text":"Alternatively, a more exotic scenario is that 'Oumuamua may be a fully operational probe sent intentionally to Earth vicinity by an alien civilization.","speaker":"Shmuel Bialy & Abraham Loeb","date":"2018-11-12","source":"Bialy & Loeb, ApJL 868 L1 (arXiv:1810.11490)","camp":"loeb","context":"The paper's most-quoted line, immediately following the lightsail-debris suggestion; verified verbatim against the arXiv full text."},{"text":"'Oumuamua was like a buoy resting in the expanse of the universe, and our solar system was like a ship that ran into it at high speed.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2021-01-26","source":"Loeb, 'Extraterrestrial: The First Sign of Intelligent Life Beyond Earth' (Houghton Mifflin Harcourt)","camp":"loeb","context":"Loeb's signature buoy analogy from his bestselling book — 'Oumuamua as a stationary object the solar system ran into, echoing its LSR-rest-frame kinematics."},{"text":"If radiation pressure is the accelerating force, then 'Oumuamua represents a new class of thin interstellar material, either produced naturally . . . or is of an artificial origin.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2021-01-26","source":"Loeb, 'Extraterrestrial' (restating the Bialy-Loeb argument)","camp":"loeb","context":"Loeb's book-form framing of the radiation-pressure argument; near-identical sentence appears in the 2018 paper. Verified via published book excerpts."},{"text":"Thus, we find no compelling evidence to favor an alien explanation for 'Oumuamua.","speaker":"The 'Oumuamua ISSI Team (Bannister, Meech, Knight, Fitzsimmons et al.)","date":"2019-07-01","source":"'The Natural History of 'Oumuamua', Nature Astronomy 3, 594-602 (arXiv:1907.01910)","camp":"official","context":"The 14-expert International Space Science Institute team's formal verdict, closing the section that assessed the artificial hypothesis; verified verbatim from the paper PDF."},{"text":"Assertions that 'Oumuamua may be artificial are not justified when the wide body of current knowledge about solar system minor bodies and planetary formation is considered.","speaker":"The 'Oumuamua ISSI Team","date":"2019-07-01","source":"'The Natural History of 'Oumuamua', Nature Astronomy — Conclusions section","camp":"official","context":"The paper's concluding rebuttal sentence; verified verbatim from the arXiv PDF Conclusions."},{"text":"We have never seen anything like 'Oumuamua in our solar system. It's really a mystery still.","speaker":"Matthew Knight","date":"2019-07-01","source":"University of Maryland press release for the ISSI Nature Astronomy paper","camp":"official","context":"ISSI team co-lead acknowledging the object remains strange even while ruling it natural — the honest tension in the official position."},{"text":"The alien spacecraft hypothesis is a fun idea, but our analysis suggests there is a whole host of natural phenomena that could explain it.","speaker":"Matthew Knight","date":"2019-07-01","source":"University of Maryland / ISSI team press release (also carried by Sci-News, EurekAlert)","camp":"official","context":"The ISSI team's public-facing dismissal of the Loeb lightsail interpretation."},{"text":"This unusually large variation in brightness means that the object is highly elongated: about ten times as long as it is wide, with a complex, convoluted shape.","speaker":"Karen Meech","date":"2017-11-20","source":"ESO press release eso1737, 'ESO Observations Show First Interstellar Asteroid is Like Nothing Seen Before' (VLT results, Meech et al. Nature 552)","camp":"official","context":"Discovery-team lead describing the record 10:1 elongation from the VLT lightcurve; she added it was 'completely inert, without the faintest hint of dust around it.'"},{"text":"I think it's a remnant from another solar system. It's just something that happened to run into us, and we were very lucky to have been operating the telescope that night.","speaker":"Robert Weryk","date":"2018-11-06","source":"CBC News interview, 'Oumuamua not an alien probe, says scientist who discovered it' (also carried by Fox News 2018-11-12)","camp":"official","context":"The Pan-STARRS astronomer who discovered 'Oumuamua, dismissing the Bialy-Loeb lightsail idea as 'wild speculation' — 'we actually believe that's not true based on the data we obtained.'"},{"text":"I saw a moving object in the frame, it moved in a direction that was slightly different from that of main asteroids.","speaker":"Gennadiy Borisov","date":"2019-09-16","source":"RIA Novosti interview (translated from Russian; as cited on the 2I/Borisov Wikipedia discovery account)","camp":"official","context":"The Crimean amateur astronomer describing the 2019-08-30 pre-dawn discovery of 2I/Borisov with his self-built 0.65 m telescope at MARGO observatory. English is a translation."},{"text":"Every amateur … dreams of discovering something unusual and extraordinary. When you manage to do that, you feel joy and happiness, and you set a new task.","speaker":"Gennadiy Borisov","date":"2019-12-08","source":"Space.com Q&A with Meghan Bartels (email interview via Roscosmos), published on 2I/Borisov's perihelion day","camp":"official","context":"Borisov on what the discovery meant to him, published the day his comet reached perihelion (~2 AU)."},{"text":"2I/Borisov could represent the first truly pristine comet ever observed.","speaker":"Stefano Bagnulo","date":"2021-03-30","source":"ESO press release eso2106, 'First interstellar comet may be the most pristine ever found' (VLT polarimetry; Bagnulo et al., Nature Communications)","camp":"official","context":"The 'most pristine comet' line: VLT polarimetry showed 2I/Borisov more pristine than Hale-Bopp, apparently never having passed close to any star — the classic-comet control case to 'Oumuamua."},{"text":"1I/'Oumuamua is anomalous relative to 2I/Borisov and 3I/ATLAS … it had an extreme disk-like shape, it exhibited a non-gravitational acceleration and it did not show any evidence for cometary activity.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-07-09","source":"Loeb, 'Origin and Age of Three Interstellar Objects: 1I/'Oumuamua, 2I/Borisov, and 3I/ATLAS', Medium essay","camp":"loeb","context":"Loeb's retrospective three-object comparison after 3I/ATLAS's discovery: Borisov and ATLAS behave like comets, 'Oumuamua still stands apart. Ellipsis trims 'not only because it is much younger than they are.' Verified verbatim."},{"text":"3I/ATLAS is most likely a comet of natural origin, but there are 8 anomalies that endow it with a rank of 4 on the Loeb scale.","speaker":"Avi Loeb","date":"2025-12-21","source":"Loeb, 'Updating the Loeb Classification Scale of 3I/ATLAS', Medium (the Loeb scale itself defined in Trivedi & Loeb, arXiv:2508.09167, Aug 2025)","camp":"loeb","context":"Loeb applying his 0-10 anomaly scale to 3I/ATLAS; the companion Loeb-scale framework retroactively treats 2I/Borisov as a rank-0 natural baseline while 'Oumuamua keeps an elevated rank. Verified verbatim from the Medium essay."}]};
